WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.420
- Good afternoon, I'd
like to welcome everyone

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:14.420
to our Fourth Endeavors
Colloquium this semester.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:19.060
And it's a glorious day
for several reasons,

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.380
not least of which being
that we're around the bend

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:23.890
with the election.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:26.290
And it's really great to come together

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:30.840
and think about the
meaning and power of art

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.690
to guide us through moments like these.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.490
I'm Jacqueline Goldsby I'm chair

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.360
of African-American Studies here at Yale.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:40.910
And I want to point out that
we have two more sessions

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:44.910
to present in this series of discussions.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.700
We've been holding off semester

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.574
next Thursday on November
19th, which is a Thursday.

00:00:51.574 --> 00:00:53.280
This is a special day.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:58.280
But on Thursday, November
19th at 11:45 to 1:15,

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.770
one of our newest faculty members

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:03.810
in African-American Studies
Professor Phillip Goff

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.950
will detail the groundbreaking
work he's been doing

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.640
directing the Center for Policing Equity.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.920
And there's a way in
which the center's work

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.980
has kind of aptly summed
up in one of its models

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.970
doing data science for racial justice.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.700
And Professor Goff will
unpack what that phrase,

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.700
that idea means and how his
team has put that principle

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:29.610
into scholarly action
and political activism

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.130
around the politics of policing.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:33.330
So that will be next Thursday.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:37.550
And then on December 10th,
I believe it is, yes.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:42.030
Another Thursday from 11:45 to 1:15,

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:45.453
another new colleague
Professor Elizabeth Hinton,

00:01:46.565 --> 00:01:47.830
a wonderful, brilliant historian

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:50.290
will team up with a long time ally

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.140
of African-American Studies,

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.690
Professor Tracy Meares of the Law School.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:56.830
And they're going to map out
the work that's happening

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:58.300
at the Justice Collaboratory,

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:01.060
which is a research and
policy action center

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:03.510
organized by law school faculty.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.110
So those are the next two events,

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:07.633
and we hope to see all of you there.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:10.610
The department chose to dedicate

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:12.380
the semester's colloquium series

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.540
to issues that would be decisive

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.140
to the outcome of the
presidential election and beyond

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.360
because we know our work is not done.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.790
Even though the election
went in the direction

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.943
I hope most of us want it to go.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.120
But just to review for those of you

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.290
who haven't been with us these past weeks,

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.580
the challenges of securing voting rights

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:37.010
and theorizing new models of citizenship,

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:39.840
which we got to do with LaTosha Brown,

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.170
who directs, co-directs,
co-founded Black Voters Matter

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:49.170
this tremendously still
alive with us as an issue.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.980
The delivery of justice
advancing healthcare

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:55.750
to the formerly incarcerated,
their families and communities

00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:00.010
was an amazing discussion
that we held with Emily Wang,

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.960
who directs the Center
for Health and Justice

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:04.900
out of the Yale School of Medicine.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.370
And today, again, we're
going to be thinking

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:10.790
about the enduring and
evolving role of art

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:14.240
in making social justice
thinkable and actionable

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.670
with our extraordinary panel

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:17.320
who you'll meet in just a minute.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.020
I wanna thank our co-sponsor

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:23.280
the Center for Study of Race, Indigeneity,

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.420
and Transnational Migration

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:27.730
who's been co-sponsoring
these events with us

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:31.080
and the Director of RITM
Professor Steve Pitti

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.550
and its Associate Director Matthew Tanico

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:34.990
have been extraordinary colleagues

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:37.290
with us to make all of this come together.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.080
I wanna thank them.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.300
I also wanna thank our staff, Vanessa Epps

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.530
who's been incredibly diligent

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:46.510
of just stepping into
the work, publicizing

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:48.590
and getting these Zoom links out

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.530
for all of us to congregate here together.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.920
I wanna thank her for that work.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.910
And Emmanuel Bissell,
who's here with us today

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.230
managing of the Zoom room to make sure

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.500
that all the connections
work the way they should.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:02.200
I wanna thank them both

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:05.160
for their support of this initiative.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:06.320
And now I wanna turn it over

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:09.210
to my colleague, Professor
Crystal Feimster,

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.590
who's done a brilliant
job organizing the series

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:13.500
and she'll introduce today's panel.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.580
- Thank you, Jackie.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.360
And I also wanna add my gratitude

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:22.870
to both Vanessa and Emmanuel
for all their support

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:25.820
and making this happen and working with me

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:29.356
and just making sure
everything runs smoothly.

00:04:29.356 --> 00:04:31.480
(indistinct) is particularly Vanessa.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:32.313
I haven't said this,

00:04:32.313 --> 00:04:35.710
but helping to design the beautiful flyers

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.763
that we have for this series.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:41.497
Vanessa put a lot of work into that

00:04:41.497 --> 00:04:43.977
and I think they're really beautiful.

00:04:43.977 --> 00:04:45.700
So I want to just give her a shout out

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:47.283
for her work on that front.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:52.760
But also want to thank all
of you for being here today.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:57.760
I know like myself and
many others retired,

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:03.423
but I think there is joy
and much to celebrate.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.640
I know that I been inspired
in the last couple of days

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.070
in way that I haven't been
able to find inspiration

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:12.923
in a while.

00:05:16.418 --> 00:05:19.080
And I think that part of our celebration,

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.403
I feel like this work,

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:23.640
these artists that are with us today

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:26.617
are gonna inspire us

00:05:26.617 --> 00:05:29.890
with not just their words and their art,

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:33.690
but with their presence and remind us that

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:35.950
the ways in which we can find beauty

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:38.740
and joy in the struggle.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:42.760
So I am so grateful that they
are gonna be with us today

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:44.803
and holding this space for us.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:47.690
Before I introduce them,

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:50.233
I wanna remind folks about the format.

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:55.530
We will allow our participants engage

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:59.760
in a robust and conversation
for about 45 minutes.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:01.210
Around four o'clock,

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:04.610
I will sort of give them the nod

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:07.720
to go ahead and start
opening up for questions.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:10.460
We take the questions in the chat.

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:12.000
Ideally, we would love for you

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.000
to send those questions,
comments to Claudia Rankine

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:19.410
in the private chat,

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.800
and she will sort through the questions

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:24.089
and really present them

00:06:24.089 --> 00:06:29.089
our two gifted artists,
scholars, intellectuals

00:06:29.453 --> 00:06:32.310
to respond to.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:35.220
I'll ask that you do that

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:37.760
and make sure you keep your mikes on mute

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:39.860
throughout the conversation.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:42.580
And on that note, I have the great honor

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:47.580
of introducing three wonderful
scholars and artists.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.183
I wanna begin with my
colleague Claudia Rankine.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:54.850
She is the author of five books of poetry,

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:58.170
including "Citizen" and "American lyric",

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:00.770
and "Don't Let Me Be Lonely",

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:04.420
three plays, including
"Help" and "The White Card",

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:06.530
her recent collection of essays

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:08.960
was just published by Graywolf Press

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.390
in 2020 titled "Just Us"
and "American Conversation".

00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:16.760
And in 2016, Rankine co-founded

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:19.690
the Racial Imaginary Institute

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:22.510
and among her numerous awards and honors

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:24.000
Rankine is the recipient

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.630
of the Bobbitt National Prize for Poetry,

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:29.880
the Poets and Writers
Jackson Poetry Prize,

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:34.390
and a Fellowships from
the Guggenheim Foundation

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:35.700
and the MacArthur Foundation

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:39.090
and the National Endowment of
the Arts to just name a few.

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:42.050
And we're really lucky and honored

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:45.250
to have her leading
this conversation today.

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:47.963
We also have Reginald Dwayne Betts,

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:52.020
one of a friend and a colleague

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:56.170
and a co-parent in this
community that we call New Haven.

00:07:56.170 --> 00:08:00.800
So it's a real treat to
have him in Athenaeum

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:03.270
in conversation with us today.

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:07.340
He's a renowned and published
poet speaker and leader

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:09.800
in public defense and advocacy.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:12.310
Betts is the recipient of numerous awards,

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:15.670
including the 2018 Guggenheim Fellowship

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:17.850
and the New American Fellowship.

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:20.840
Betts' writing has been
featured in the New York Times,

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:23.670
The New Yorker and the Washington Post.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:27.260
Betts is currently leading
The Million Book Projects

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:30.530
with my colleague, Elizabeth Hinton

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:33.930
and colleagues over in the law school,

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:37.870
under Tracy Meares' leadership over there,

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:40.070
and then initiative which aims

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:43.430
to transform the role of
literature and libraries

00:08:43.430 --> 00:08:45.710
in the lives of people in prison

00:08:47.766 --> 00:08:48.950
is in the making.

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:50.533
They're doing that work now.

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:55.080
And finally, I wanna
introduce Titus Kaphar,

00:08:55.080 --> 00:09:00.080
also a friend and a member
of this New Haven Community

00:09:02.485 --> 00:09:05.690
that we're really lucky to have him here.

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:08.440
He's an artist whose painting sculptures

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:10.330
and installations examine

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:11.990
the history of representation

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:14.750
by transforming it styles and mediums

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:18.280
with formal innovations to
emphasize the physicality

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:21.970
and the dimensionality of the canvas

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:24.350
and materials themselves.

00:09:24.350 --> 00:09:27.833
Kaphar received an MFA
from the Yale school of Art

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:30.510
and as a distinguished recipient

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:32.330
of numerous prizes and awards,

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:35.080
including a 2014 Gwendolyn Knight

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:37.030
and Jacob Lawrence Fellowship

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:40.580
and 2018 art for Justice Fund grant,

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:43.790
and the 2018 Rappaport Prize.

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:48.184
Kaphar is painting Analogous Colors 2020.

00:09:48.184 --> 00:09:49.570
It was featured on the cover

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:54.140
of the June 15th, 2020
issue of Time Magazine,

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:57.010
which included a report
on the protest sparked

00:09:57.010 --> 00:10:01.410
by George Floyd's killing at the
hands of Minneapolis police.

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:04.310
Kaphar's work is included
in the collections

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:06.160
of the Museum of Modern Art,

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:08.850
of course, the Yale University Art Gallery

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:10.710
among many others.

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:13.407
Kaphar has two forthcoming exhibits,

00:10:13.407 --> 00:10:16.670
and one of them actually
might be open now in New York,

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:19.340
but he can talk about
that, and in Belgium.

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:20.710
And on that note,

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:23.350
I'm gonna turn it over to Dwayne

00:10:23.350 --> 00:10:26.710
who is going to share some images with us

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:29.593
so that we can all be on the same page.

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.750
- Cool, yeah, how's everybody doing?

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:36.200
Oh this is Zoom y'all
can't even talk back to me.

00:10:38.844 --> 00:10:39.690
I'ma share my screen

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:41.970
and then will walk you
through a few images

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:44.343
and then we will start the conversation.

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:46.750
Thank everybody for coming though.

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:49.930
So what you see here is
I'm just gonna walk through

00:10:49.930 --> 00:10:51.410
some of the pieces

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:53.650
that went to Pleading Freedom Exhibit.

00:10:53.650 --> 00:10:55.330
So this is one of Titus's pieces,

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:57.130
and really I'm just gonna
show you the images.

00:10:57.130 --> 00:10:59.947
I'm not going to try to
explain it or talk about them.

00:10:59.947 --> 00:11:01.547
I'm just gonna run through them.

00:11:53.582 --> 00:11:54.832
And those are the pieces.

00:11:57.580 --> 00:12:00.640
- Thanks Dwayne, hello
everyone, good afternoon.

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:02.990
It's I was up on a rock today.

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:04.230
It's a beautiful day.

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:06.993
So it's nice to spend
the afternoon with you.

00:12:09.882 --> 00:12:11.380
And I bring up being on the rock

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:13.030
because I went on a walk the other day

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:16.120
and I was telling someone
that I was working with

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:22.420
that I was going to talk
to Dwayne and Titus today.

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:25.410
And they said, Oh, Dwayne and Titus,

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:27.020
they met at St. Thomas.

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:29.510
I don't even know if this is true,

00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:32.700
but the story I got was that

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:35.780
because their children
went to the same school

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:37.620
they became friends.

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:39.883
Is this at all true either of you?

00:12:42.629 --> 00:12:44.030
- I can't see Titus, but no it's not true.

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:45.592
- It's not true.

00:12:45.592 --> 00:12:46.570
- It's okay though.

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:48.760
Everybody has to have a
mythology created about them,

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:51.020
so we could just say that.

00:12:52.398 --> 00:12:54.445
- Now I met them.

00:12:54.445 --> 00:12:55.750
- How you met them.

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:59.316
I liked that story
because I liked the fact

00:12:59.316 --> 00:13:01.930
that it was the generations

00:13:01.930 --> 00:13:04.856
that your kids were bringing together.

00:13:04.856 --> 00:13:05.970
You two great guys

00:13:06.890 --> 00:13:09.123
and amazing artists in the world.

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:11.330
But okay, it's not true.

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:12.193
So there it goes.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:16.570
I'm gonna have to go back to that person

00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:18.470
and ask them where they got that from.

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:22.980
The Pleading Freedoms Exhibit,

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:25.490
which is the sort of
catalyst for this talk

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.130
is a talk that I thought we could rename

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:31.510
this discussion in this way,

00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:33.400
that our conversation

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:36.420
is going to be about racials, redactions

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:40.130
and other critical creative
collaborations and endeavors

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:41.843
by the both of you.

00:13:44.689 --> 00:13:48.743
I wanted to begin by
thinking about a racial.

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:54.280
There are many ways to enter into that,

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:59.280
and it's been a very sort of hot topic

00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:03.610
because we've had in
circulation a lot of videos

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:09.570
around black death and black suffering.

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:13.600
And so I was really
interested in beginning

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:18.600
with the considerations of how each of you

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:24.080
sort of walk the line
between artists and activists

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:29.080
or writer and activist relative
to the creation of work.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:32.723
And then the second part
of that question would be,

00:14:35.726 --> 00:14:38.426
how does the act of engaging
either racial or redactor

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:44.673
from archival materials
play into the practice?

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:48.890
- Yeah, that's an interesting question.

00:14:52.703 --> 00:14:53.570
I wonder about the first piece

00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:56.240
you had on the Times Magazine cover Titus

00:14:56.240 --> 00:14:58.220
'cause I sort of feel like,

00:14:58.220 --> 00:14:59.635
at least for me,

00:14:59.635 --> 00:15:01.574
it was a bit of a reluctance
actually to try to make my art

00:15:01.574 --> 00:15:04.630
immediately reflect in the moment

00:15:05.890 --> 00:15:07.600
to try to speak to the immediate moment.

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:09.010
That concerns that were on my mind

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:11.420
as a citizen in this country.

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:13.807
And I remember the actually,

00:15:13.807 --> 00:15:16.920
you had Aragon, you had Michael Brown,

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:19.020
and you just had his wave,

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:22.400
and I'll just speak to
the two specific poems

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:23.880
and how they came about.

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:25.280
And they came about,

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:29.410
as first, I was resistant
trying to be the poet

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:31.800
that writes about the
things happening right now,

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:34.378
'cause in some ways you end up getting

00:15:34.378 --> 00:15:35.728
a slogan instead of a poem.

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:38.567
And I was writing verses at Arigna

00:15:38.567 --> 00:15:41.330
and my full intent was to try to engage

00:15:41.330 --> 00:15:44.260
with this question of violence
and how violence was working

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:46.100
within our country and
within our communities

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:49.400
and specifically within
the world of incarceration.

00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:51.170
And I understood it and my friends

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:57.000
and I just remember driving
to Rutgers for a talk.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:58.190
And when I came back,

00:15:58.190 --> 00:16:00.770
I think somebody got
acquitted on the way back

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:03.831
and we got awarded acquittal or something.

00:16:03.831 --> 00:16:05.800
And it was just in that moment

00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:09.330
that I ended up just having to change

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:10.770
one of the poems that I written,

00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:12.470
I had to rethink it

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:15.280
and really seriously
take into considerations

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:16.690
what it was that I was trying to say.

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:18.290
And I know I had one line in it,

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:21.310
I sort of framed a conversation,

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:22.667
but I knew I had one line in it.

00:16:22.667 --> 00:16:24.257
"If somebody is throwing a trash can

00:16:24.257 --> 00:16:27.117
"through Sal's window,
calling that revolution

00:16:27.117 --> 00:16:31.387
"while the body still roll off
the blocks into the prisons

00:16:31.387 --> 00:16:32.937
"and into the graveyards."

00:16:34.828 --> 00:16:36.510
And so for me, the
whole process was an act

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:39.150
of like a reluctance of
reluctance of reluctance

00:16:39.150 --> 00:16:42.610
until so much a resistant
a moment forced me

00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:44.040
into the moment and allowed me, I think,

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:46.180
to get into the moment in a different way.

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:48.370
And to me, race poem that
I wrote was the same.

00:16:48.370 --> 00:16:49.400
It was sorta like,

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:51.293
I'm not writing a poem about this.

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:54.570
I'm not gonna write a poem about

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:56.980
the police murder into my race

00:16:56.980 --> 00:16:59.180
until I told a friend to write a poem

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:00.760
about something they
would never read in public

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:02.370
and at the law school to get this wall

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:03.460
where everybody could talk.

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:05.500
And like I wrote this poem

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:07.090
and I was so mad at the discussions

00:17:07.090 --> 00:17:10.401
that were going on around
what was happening.

00:17:10.401 --> 00:17:12.690
I have wrote the poem a few weeks ago

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:14.460
and I was never gonna show anybody.

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:16.810
And then I just posted it
to the entire law school

00:17:16.810 --> 00:17:18.510
as if that's how you publish a poem.

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:20.810
And I thought that is
how you publish a poem.

00:17:20.810 --> 00:17:21.970
When you write a piece

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:24.110
and you want the community where you live

00:17:24.110 --> 00:17:26.419
to hear everything that you got to say.

00:17:26.419 --> 00:17:28.380
ANd the poem was kind of raw, actually,

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:30.250
I hadn't even really
worked on it too much.

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:31.340
It was just there.

00:17:31.340 --> 00:17:32.590
And it was the same thing.

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:33.903
How do you...

00:17:33.903 --> 00:17:35.540
And in fact, I wrote that
to speak about St. T's,

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:36.410
I was really thinking about

00:17:36.410 --> 00:17:38.393
what it meant to drive my kids to school

00:17:38.393 --> 00:17:39.557
in a really privileged school

00:17:39.557 --> 00:17:41.040
and a really privileged place.

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:43.420
And I was thinking about
how we got a whole...

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:45.010
We got far too many black folks

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:47.000
who own a kind of suffering

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:50.200
and fear that their lives don't embody.

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:52.320
And I felt it was something
grotesque about it.

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:54.379
I still think there's
something grotesque about that.

00:17:54.379 --> 00:17:56.950
And it's like, how do I work
my way through these issues

00:17:56.950 --> 00:18:00.040
while acknowledging that it
is a fair amount of privilege

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:03.030
that I carry every day into the world.

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:05.280
And so part of that poem was
me trying to confront that

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:08.110
and trying to say, you
know, despite the privilege,

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:10.930
I'm still a black man
driving his two children,

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:12.690
his two black sons to school

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:16.120
and the death of the
murder of a 12 year old.

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:18.163
This was playing a repeat in my head.

00:18:19.040 --> 00:18:21.760
So anyway, these poems for
me come out of a reluctance

00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:24.340
and I'm not really an
activist in any kind of way.

00:18:24.340 --> 00:18:26.740
I try to get people I know how to prison.

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:29.640
I've decided that I just need
to simplify my own terms.

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:30.880
And so I'm in no way an activist really.

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:33.690
I just try to get people
I know how to prison.

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:37.150
- Titus before you go,
don't answer the question.

00:18:37.150 --> 00:18:40.283
I just wanna follow up
with Dwayne on one thing.

00:18:40.283 --> 00:18:43.160
When you have written so
much about your own life

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:44.760
as somebody who...

00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:46.650
Your last book is called "Felon".

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:50.010
So in some ways Dwayne
aren't you turning yourself

00:18:58.216 --> 00:19:02.500
into a metaphor of the
system of mass incarceration

00:19:02.500 --> 00:19:06.000
so that the subject of your work

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:11.000
becomes not, it's both personal
and public at the same time,

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:16.633
and as personal as you get.

00:19:17.523 --> 00:19:20.290
The piece about in the Times

00:19:20.290 --> 00:19:21.780
where you bring up your mother's story

00:19:21.780 --> 00:19:23.920
as well as a rape victim.

00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:26.250
It seems to me that this idea

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:29.570
that you're reluctant
to move into the realm

00:19:34.365 --> 00:19:37.567
of the devastated and
victimize black person

00:19:40.030 --> 00:19:44.450
within our society might be useful for you

00:19:44.450 --> 00:19:46.410
in the creation of things,

00:19:46.410 --> 00:19:50.720
but it's not necessarily true
in terms of where you're going

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:52.590
in terms of your subject.

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:54.910
- It depends 'cause it took me
10 years to write that piece

00:19:54.910 --> 00:19:56.943
that I just published in the Times.

00:19:58.030 --> 00:20:01.037
A literal and 10 years to...

00:20:01.037 --> 00:20:02.560
And really you could say 15

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:05.190
in terms of knowing my
mother's experience,

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:07.360
and then even beginning to write about it.

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:08.950
And if you go through the trace,

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:10.440
the through line of my work,

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:13.930
you'll find me dealing
with sexual assault a lot.

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:16.460
And you'll find me dealing
with domestic violence a lot.

00:20:16.460 --> 00:20:18.500
But that's me taking half steps

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:20.200
to the thing that I
wanted or needed to say.

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:22.030
So I would argue that,

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:23.620
it's like, yes and no.

00:20:23.620 --> 00:20:25.360
I would argue that...

00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:28.590
And even in the poems I
write in first person a lot,

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:30.190
but part of it is I believe that

00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:32.003
I need to figure out a way

00:20:32.003 --> 00:20:35.440
how to situate all of it in the lyric ad,

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:37.810
because I think anything
else ends up being me

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:39.920
unwilling to embrace

00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:41.650
some of the hardest things I write about

00:20:41.650 --> 00:20:43.600
so that when somebody is inflicting pain,

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:46.090
it's always somebody else
that's doing it, it's not me.

00:20:46.090 --> 00:20:47.930
So even when you read 'Felon",

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:49.510
it is a fair amount of it that's me,

00:20:49.510 --> 00:20:50.560
but it's also a fair amount

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:52.890
of it is written in the
first person is not me.

00:20:52.890 --> 00:20:54.940
And most of those poems.

00:20:54.940 --> 00:20:58.470
I hope they feel fresh and
I hope they feel immediate,

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:01.717
but a lot of that work has
taken like years to get to.

00:21:01.717 --> 00:21:04.240
And some of it is not about me at all.

00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:07.120
But what you said that
whole heartedly agree with

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:08.980
is the point is the juxtaposition

00:21:08.980 --> 00:21:10.240
out of public and private

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:13.120
to create something that I
feel is like fully political.

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:14.720
But I feel like that work...

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:16.360
I try to honor the work that activists do

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:17.600
and just sort of like acknowledge

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:19.410
that that's not what it is that I do.

00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:23.110
And frequently, I'm just
lucky that my work is...

00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:26.020
Like this conversation about
Harris and sexual assault

00:21:26.020 --> 00:21:28.430
and also getting friends out of prison.

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:31.230
Like fundamentally that piece
is talking about two people

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:33.280
who I can name and I can pull up

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:35.170
a Springshare parole
package that I'm working on

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:37.080
to get them out of prison.

00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:39.830
Like that work feels like
it's at a moment right now,

00:21:39.830 --> 00:21:42.810
but I've been working on those
cases for five, six years.

00:21:42.810 --> 00:21:44.490
I've been working on trying
to get them out of prison

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:46.410
before Virginia brought parole back.

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:49.510
And so it is a way in which
you can read it and think,

00:21:49.510 --> 00:21:50.870
Oh man, Dwayne is on point.

00:21:50.870 --> 00:21:52.183
He is right in the moment.

00:21:53.101 --> 00:21:53.934
And the truth is no,

00:21:53.934 --> 00:21:56.560
I'm just lost in the moment
that I got lost in...

00:21:56.560 --> 00:21:59.963
When I called Jack somebody
on December 7th, 1996.

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:04.010
- Thank you, what about you Titus?

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:06.840
How do you walk the line
between being an artist

00:22:06.840 --> 00:22:10.200
and an activist since
your work speaks directly

00:22:11.460 --> 00:22:14.850
as timeless as the pieces are.

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:16.320
They're still historical,

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:19.010
they're still critiquing,

00:22:19.010 --> 00:22:20.490
they're still engaged

00:22:20.490 --> 00:22:23.923
in many of the vibrant
questions we have now.

00:22:25.970 --> 00:22:29.470
- I think one of the things
that Dwayne is getting at is,

00:22:29.470 --> 00:22:30.303
and one of the things

00:22:30.303 --> 00:22:32.880
I think we're eventually
going to get at is

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:35.260
just this idea that the
political is personal

00:22:35.260 --> 00:22:37.212
and the personal political.

00:22:37.212 --> 00:22:40.162
I think eventually we're gonna
come around to that somehow.

00:22:43.920 --> 00:22:45.920
The piece that Dwayne referred to,

00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:48.570
the first painting that was in the Time

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:54.220
was editorialize as something

00:22:54.220 --> 00:22:55.923
that was very much about Ferguson,

00:22:57.253 --> 00:22:59.630
but Ferguson was a secondary,

00:22:59.630 --> 00:23:03.010
it's the B role to the A role

00:23:03.853 --> 00:23:06.230
in terms of what the painting is about.

00:23:06.230 --> 00:23:07.650
The painting is about

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:09.730
me getting stopped by the
police with my brother.

00:23:09.730 --> 00:23:10.970
That's what the painting is about.

00:23:10.970 --> 00:23:13.180
The painting is about
my personal experience,

00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:15.700
being a Chelsea, I've shared this a lot

00:23:17.284 --> 00:23:19.970
where the police officers
stopped me and my brother,

00:23:19.970 --> 00:23:21.230
while we were walking down the street

00:23:21.230 --> 00:23:23.430
and put us up against
a wall, put guns on us

00:23:25.144 --> 00:23:27.793
and accused me of stealing
art out of my own shop.

00:23:33.055 --> 00:23:37.080
It's personal, and I think
for me that's the hard part,

00:23:37.080 --> 00:23:39.653
I'm like what you were
saying before Dwayne is like,

00:23:40.530 --> 00:23:43.103
when at that point in my practice,

00:23:43.980 --> 00:23:46.210
actually, that was point in my practice

00:23:50.611 --> 00:23:52.800
where my work was no longer private

00:23:54.316 --> 00:23:55.680
'cause up until then,

00:23:55.680 --> 00:23:58.870
I'm just a dude in the studio making stuff

00:23:58.870 --> 00:24:01.210
that nobody really cares about anyway.

00:24:01.210 --> 00:24:04.060
I'm just trying to figure out
what's going on in my head.

00:24:05.092 --> 00:24:08.580
And then that work came into the world

00:24:10.311 --> 00:24:15.170
and all of a sudden I
found myself being public

00:24:15.170 --> 00:24:16.780
in a way that anybody who knows me

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:19.643
knows that I'm not like
super comfortable with.

00:24:20.840 --> 00:24:24.090
And so I think what
Dwayne may be resisting

00:24:24.090 --> 00:24:29.090
is this idea of us as activists symbols.

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:33.090
There's a whole like
symbolism that goes along

00:24:33.090 --> 00:24:36.973
with that idea that I'm just
not in no way interested in it.

00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:40.400
I'm interested in the work.

00:24:40.400 --> 00:24:42.360
I'm interested in justice.

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:46.223
I'm interested in understanding
my role in the world,

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:52.880
but I read too much history
and seeing too much history

00:24:53.260 --> 00:24:55.150
to know that...

00:24:55.150 --> 00:24:56.600
To be comfortable with the way

00:24:56.600 --> 00:24:59.300
in which people wanna put you

00:24:59.300 --> 00:25:02.232
on a some kind of a different pedestal

00:25:02.232 --> 00:25:03.570
as an activist,

00:25:03.570 --> 00:25:06.160
just to wait for you to
slip up and fall down

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:10.843
and then like have some overjoyed
expression when you fall.

00:25:14.410 --> 00:25:16.190
The other thing I want to say is like,

00:25:16.190 --> 00:25:18.690
no, that story is not true
about (indistinct) but,

00:25:22.150 --> 00:25:23.890
and I know Jason Stanley is on the line

00:25:23.890 --> 00:25:27.410
who always wants to take credit
for us meeting each other,

00:25:27.410 --> 00:25:29.153
but I'm not gonna let you do that.

00:25:30.319 --> 00:25:33.410
But what I will say about
this story is our friendship

00:25:33.410 --> 00:25:35.963
is rooted in our fatherhood.

00:25:37.370 --> 00:25:42.370
I think that Dwayne and I
like our connection is deeper

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:45.940
because we are both black fathers

00:25:45.940 --> 00:25:48.090
trying to figure out
how to raise our kids.

00:25:49.544 --> 00:25:52.840
And we are raising our kids in realities

00:25:52.840 --> 00:25:56.610
so different from the
reality that we grew up in.

00:25:56.610 --> 00:26:01.610
Our kids have no
understanding of the struggles

00:26:02.762 --> 00:26:05.470
that we went through
when we were their age.

00:26:05.470 --> 00:26:08.470
And in some ways we don't
necessarily want them

00:26:08.470 --> 00:26:09.910
to fully understand that,

00:26:09.910 --> 00:26:12.260
they shouldn't have to go through that.

00:26:12.260 --> 00:26:14.450
But I think that that's a fundamental part

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:16.840
of our relationship.

00:26:16.840 --> 00:26:19.230
We talk a lot about being dads

00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:20.820
and what that means.

00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:22.020
Even though that wasn't,

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:24.980
we didn't meet at St. Thomas in that way,

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:27.040
both of our kids go to St. Thomas.

00:26:27.040 --> 00:26:28.590
And so it has become

00:26:28.590 --> 00:26:30.690
a very important part of our relationship.

00:26:32.230 --> 00:26:33.313
- Thank you for that.

00:26:34.907 --> 00:26:37.887
I also wanna pick up on this reluctance

00:26:39.770 --> 00:26:44.350
around the naming of the
work as in any way activist.

00:26:44.350 --> 00:26:47.430
Because I wonder,

00:26:47.430 --> 00:26:50.910
and I wonder this from
my own personal position,

00:26:50.910 --> 00:26:54.120
there has been a tendency to name work

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:57.453
that is done by black
people as political work.

00:26:58.799 --> 00:27:01.140
And that has been out there as a way

00:27:01.140 --> 00:27:03.130
to denigrate the work in an odd way.

00:27:03.130 --> 00:27:07.340
Both is flattering and saying that,

00:27:07.340 --> 00:27:11.010
oh, you're involved in what's going on,

00:27:11.010 --> 00:27:14.370
you're on the cutting edge
of politics in America.

00:27:14.370 --> 00:27:15.240
But on the other way,

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:17.120
it's a way of saying it's not white work,

00:27:17.120 --> 00:27:18.670
it's not transcendent work,

00:27:18.670 --> 00:27:20.430
it's not work that is able

00:27:20.430 --> 00:27:24.280
to really address the human condition

00:27:24.280 --> 00:27:27.570
and how all of us are one thing

00:27:27.570 --> 00:27:29.670
and blah blah blah blah blah

00:27:29.670 --> 00:27:34.670
as if there is a lack of
investment in whiteness

00:27:35.640 --> 00:27:37.040
in the work of white people.

00:27:42.916 --> 00:27:44.310
Often I see artists

00:27:44.310 --> 00:27:46.490
who are of color

00:27:46.490 --> 00:27:51.180
running away from that way
of talking about their work.

00:27:51.180 --> 00:27:56.180
And I wonder how much we think about,

00:27:56.800 --> 00:28:00.620
or fear the idea that people will use it

00:28:00.620 --> 00:28:03.880
as a redactive measure around your work,

00:28:03.880 --> 00:28:08.200
whether it's the writing or the paintings.

00:28:08.200 --> 00:28:09.744
- Yeah, I think the first,

00:28:09.744 --> 00:28:12.580
I just re-read "Please
Don't Let Me Be Lonely"

00:28:12.580 --> 00:28:13.493
a few months ago.

00:28:14.470 --> 00:28:15.303
And I'm like,

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:18.140
why don't I keep editing please.

00:28:18.140 --> 00:28:20.231
- Put you put please, it's a song.

00:28:20.231 --> 00:28:21.903
- It is a song.

00:28:23.768 --> 00:28:25.180
It may be-

00:28:25.180 --> 00:28:26.840
- You read that book and?

00:28:26.840 --> 00:28:31.803
- And what I was thinking is Sean Bell

00:28:34.202 --> 00:28:35.152
I'm a do the yellow.

00:28:36.360 --> 00:28:37.570
The work is laced with that,

00:28:37.570 --> 00:28:40.840
and it's beautiful work and
it's not work of a time,

00:28:40.840 --> 00:28:42.450
is work of time.

00:28:42.450 --> 00:28:46.400
You go back to it and it
keeps delivering a richness.

00:28:46.400 --> 00:28:48.360
If somebody's pickup "Citizen", they like,

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:50.370
yeah, you noticed this
poem of Claudia Rankine

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:52.106
is really political.

00:28:52.106 --> 00:28:52.939
And I'm like, but Claudia Rankine

00:28:52.939 --> 00:28:54.580
always been Claudia Rankine.

00:28:54.580 --> 00:28:57.030
And it's just, how do you approach

00:28:57.030 --> 00:28:59.580
the breadth and the length of the work?

00:28:59.580 --> 00:29:01.310
And I think it becomes
productive both ways.

00:29:01.310 --> 00:29:03.506
I tend to not care what white
people think about my work.

00:29:03.506 --> 00:29:05.410
Like that's not even
what I'm concerned about,

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:07.580
about how people are
productive in that matter.

00:29:07.580 --> 00:29:10.410
I'm concerned about the people who I know

00:29:10.410 --> 00:29:12.720
who think that my Kamala Harris piece

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:15.330
was supposed to say what
they wanted it to say.

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:17.420
Who feel like, Oh yeah,
I've read your piece,

00:29:17.420 --> 00:29:19.266
and what I just don't understand this.

00:29:19.266 --> 00:29:21.500
You don't know the Kamala Harris is a cop,

00:29:21.500 --> 00:29:23.420
you know who wants slogans from me.

00:29:23.420 --> 00:29:25.000
And that's why I've resisted.

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:28.320
I've resisted because man, look,

00:29:28.320 --> 00:29:29.970
I shouldn't even tell this story.

00:29:29.970 --> 00:29:31.150
I'm gonna tell it.

00:29:31.150 --> 00:29:31.983
- Hold on,

00:29:33.910 --> 00:29:36.553
go ahead, go ahead 'cause
you gonna do it anyway.

00:29:36.553 --> 00:29:38.770
- I get invited to this gig here at Yale.

00:29:38.770 --> 00:29:40.443
I'm some such and such.

00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:42.760
And I'm having lunch with the folks

00:29:42.760 --> 00:29:45.030
and they say, first story is like,

00:29:45.030 --> 00:29:47.233
it was so brave of you and your book

00:29:47.233 --> 00:29:49.933
to admit that you sexually
assaulted somebody.

00:29:51.240 --> 00:29:54.913
I was like, Oh shit, what book?

00:29:54.913 --> 00:29:57.820
And I knew the poem
they were talking about.

00:29:57.820 --> 00:29:59.280
And when I wrote the poem,

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:01.050
the people who published
the poem the first time

00:30:01.050 --> 00:30:03.090
as a "Crown" the (indistinct) "Felon".

00:30:03.090 --> 00:30:05.237
they called me, and they were like,

00:30:05.237 --> 00:30:08.950
"Do you know, in this book
you admit to raping somebody?"

00:30:08.950 --> 00:30:09.783
And I was like,

00:30:09.783 --> 00:30:13.210
I'm absolutely certain that
I didn't admit to doing it

00:30:13.210 --> 00:30:14.720
because I've never done it.

00:30:14.720 --> 00:30:15.750
And I could have told them,

00:30:15.750 --> 00:30:18.150
I'm absolutely certain that
I didn't admit to doing that

00:30:18.150 --> 00:30:19.230
because I feel like

00:30:19.230 --> 00:30:21.220
that's the worst thing on
this planet you could do

00:30:21.220 --> 00:30:22.970
given the experience
of women in my family.

00:30:22.970 --> 00:30:24.650
But I didn't want to go that route.

00:30:24.650 --> 00:30:26.850
And I only say it now
because I've written a piece

00:30:26.850 --> 00:30:29.780
and it's out there, but
this person was trying to,

00:30:29.780 --> 00:30:32.040
they needed to connect to poetry,

00:30:32.040 --> 00:30:34.620
not to something that was deeper,

00:30:34.620 --> 00:30:35.940
not to something that was raised in

00:30:35.940 --> 00:30:37.410
the same kind of questions

00:30:37.410 --> 00:30:40.422
or the right white artists
that they teach in a classroom.

00:30:40.422 --> 00:30:43.120
They needed to connect it to me.

00:30:43.120 --> 00:30:44.140
And it was no reason

00:30:44.140 --> 00:30:46.465
for them to have that
need to connect it to me.

00:30:46.465 --> 00:30:49.310
I don't read Emily Dickinson

00:30:49.310 --> 00:30:51.010
and wonder if she did
the things that she did

00:30:51.010 --> 00:30:53.590
and I just don't read poetry that way.

00:30:53.590 --> 00:30:56.240
And I know that I've
been constantly taught

00:30:56.240 --> 00:30:58.390
that you weren't supposed
to read literature that way.

00:30:58.390 --> 00:31:01.780
But it's a strange thing
in which the writing

00:31:01.780 --> 00:31:06.520
of like black Raiders gets
reduced wholly to the personal,

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:08.650
and it has no imaginative force.

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:10.369
This is what they tell you,

00:31:10.369 --> 00:31:13.477
"I wish I lived the kind of life you live

00:31:13.477 --> 00:31:15.300
"so that I could write like you."

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:17.520
You don't fucking wish
you lived the life I lived

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:19.700
and you can live the life I live

00:31:19.700 --> 00:31:21.460
and still can't write like me.

00:31:21.460 --> 00:31:23.440
But that is not what they say to Ashbury.

00:31:23.440 --> 00:31:24.940
I've never heard somebody say,

00:31:26.177 --> 00:31:29.967
"Asbury is just the profound
suffering you've experienced

00:31:29.967 --> 00:31:31.910
"allows you to be a great artist."

00:31:31.910 --> 00:31:33.960
That is not the way we approach white art.

00:31:33.960 --> 00:31:36.730
And I feel like it's not
just like the white community

00:31:36.730 --> 00:31:39.000
or the white world or a
white critics would do that.

00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:40.490
I feel like unfortunately,

00:31:40.490 --> 00:31:42.900
is many people who want
you to be a jukebox

00:31:44.020 --> 00:31:46.640
and they choose the songs
that they want you to sing

00:31:46.640 --> 00:31:48.660
and they don't read
the work as expansively

00:31:48.660 --> 00:31:50.450
to allow permission for other things

00:31:50.450 --> 00:31:51.950
to exist within that work.

00:31:51.950 --> 00:31:54.760
They don't say, why does
that poem have a speaker

00:31:54.760 --> 00:31:57.370
who says what that last poem says?

00:31:57.370 --> 00:31:59.140
'Cause that's not important.

00:31:59.140 --> 00:32:02.160
What's important is how
they could characterize me.

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:04.140
And they were dead wrong.

00:32:04.140 --> 00:32:05.250
And I'ma start saying that

00:32:05.250 --> 00:32:06.490
every time I meet a white writer,

00:32:06.490 --> 00:32:08.551
I'ma take the most absurd thing

00:32:08.551 --> 00:32:09.987
that has been written in a book and say,

00:32:09.987 --> 00:32:11.127
"I can't believe

00:32:11.127 --> 00:32:14.600
"that you feel that
way about your mother."

00:32:14.600 --> 00:32:15.770
It's just sort of absurdity,

00:32:15.770 --> 00:32:19.143
things that I would never
imagine doing that people do.

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:22.366
- First of all,

00:32:22.366 --> 00:32:23.600
you know what they said about Ashbury?

00:32:23.600 --> 00:32:25.357
They said, "We don't know what the fuck

00:32:25.357 --> 00:32:26.720
"you're writing about."

00:32:26.720 --> 00:32:30.490
They trashed his early books
because they were so obscure.

00:32:30.490 --> 00:32:32.160
And part of the reason
they were so obscure

00:32:32.160 --> 00:32:35.070
is because he was at Yale and he was gay

00:32:35.070 --> 00:32:37.270
and he couldn't admit

00:32:37.270 --> 00:32:39.240
and write from that positionality.

00:32:39.240 --> 00:32:43.960
He had to create a kind
of vague subjectivity

00:32:43.960 --> 00:32:45.360
that could move through those poems.

00:32:45.360 --> 00:32:47.603
But that's another day
and another conversation.

00:32:47.603 --> 00:32:49.950
What about you Titus?

00:32:49.950 --> 00:32:51.690
- I think it's interesting

00:32:51.690 --> 00:32:54.330
'cause there is difference
because I'm making images,

00:32:54.330 --> 00:32:57.040
I'm making pictures, words and pictures

00:32:57.040 --> 00:32:59.660
are very, very, very different.

00:32:59.660 --> 00:33:02.080
And so I make images

00:33:03.140 --> 00:33:08.140
which requires not requires
that's the wrong word,

00:33:09.690 --> 00:33:13.730
compels people to want words.

00:33:13.730 --> 00:33:17.750
And sometimes you are making images

00:33:17.750 --> 00:33:21.260
because you're trying to
visualize something and ineffable.

00:33:21.260 --> 00:33:24.850
Something that doesn't have words.

00:33:24.850 --> 00:33:26.830
I start for me.

00:33:26.830 --> 00:33:30.230
I explore all kinds of ideas, of course,

00:33:30.230 --> 00:33:33.380
but I do start from a place of like,

00:33:33.380 --> 00:33:35.360
where do I connect to the story?

00:33:35.360 --> 00:33:39.297
Whether it's like
directly autobiographical

00:33:41.888 --> 00:33:42.730
is a different thing.

00:33:42.730 --> 00:33:43.590
But for me it's like,

00:33:43.590 --> 00:33:45.760
how do I connect to the story?

00:33:45.760 --> 00:33:47.410
And that's something I think about a lot.

00:33:47.410 --> 00:33:48.360
The other thing is,

00:33:49.370 --> 00:33:51.520
right now everybody wants to talk

00:33:51.520 --> 00:33:53.343
about black artist having a moment.

00:33:54.690 --> 00:33:55.523
And it's funny to me,

00:33:55.523 --> 00:33:57.590
some black artists that they're
talking about having moments

00:33:57.590 --> 00:34:00.180
have been here for like 50 years,

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:01.783
but they're now having a moment.

00:34:03.068 --> 00:34:06.850
I'm not really interested in the...

00:34:08.060 --> 00:34:11.490
I'm not interested in
trying to make work...

00:34:11.490 --> 00:34:12.530
Let me put it this way.

00:34:12.530 --> 00:34:14.400
The revolution I'm fighting for

00:34:14.400 --> 00:34:16.450
is not one where everybody
makes the same work

00:34:16.450 --> 00:34:18.170
as Kerry James Marshall.

00:34:18.170 --> 00:34:19.540
His work as amazing,

00:34:19.540 --> 00:34:22.470
but that sort of figurative work

00:34:22.470 --> 00:34:27.470
that speaks directly about
the reality of black life

00:34:28.370 --> 00:34:29.570
is not what I'm fighting for

00:34:29.570 --> 00:34:32.580
or even people making
the work that I make.

00:34:32.580 --> 00:34:35.320
As I think about this in
the context of Next Haven.

00:34:35.320 --> 00:34:37.730
What I'm interested in is like the freedom

00:34:37.730 --> 00:34:40.090
to make whatever you wanna make.

00:34:40.090 --> 00:34:42.104
What you talked about Claudia

00:34:42.104 --> 00:34:43.280
was this idea that black folks

00:34:43.280 --> 00:34:46.733
not being able to make
something that feels universal.

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:49.360
That's really what it comes down to.

00:34:49.360 --> 00:34:51.440
If Donald Judd can make a black square

00:34:51.440 --> 00:34:53.620
and explore minimalism in that way,

00:34:53.620 --> 00:34:56.433
I think our folks should be
able to do the exact same thing.

00:35:00.930 --> 00:35:04.324
I know that in my work is
going to continue to evolve.

00:35:04.324 --> 00:35:07.783
And so I'm gonna do all
kinds of different things,

00:35:10.031 --> 00:35:12.090
but that is actually
the freedom that I want

00:35:12.090 --> 00:35:14.570
is just that ability to explore

00:35:14.570 --> 00:35:17.940
whether it's abstraction,
minimalism, figurative,

00:35:17.940 --> 00:35:19.433
whatever it may be.

00:35:22.840 --> 00:35:25.540
- That brings me to the practice.

00:35:25.540 --> 00:35:27.540
'Cause I'm really interested

00:35:28.894 --> 00:35:33.040
in the pleading freedoms process

00:35:33.040 --> 00:35:36.330
and Rauschenberg, Robert Rauschenberg,

00:35:36.330 --> 00:35:40.973
he erased de Kooning's drawings in 1953.

00:35:42.304 --> 00:35:46.200
And just for John's referred
to the idea of a racial

00:35:46.200 --> 00:35:48.023
as additive subtractions.

00:35:49.380 --> 00:35:52.780
And I really love that term

00:35:54.384 --> 00:35:56.913
we saw in the initial images,

00:35:57.915 --> 00:36:00.523
and I guess I can see
them behind you Dwayne,

00:36:01.740 --> 00:36:05.660
that sense of going back into the archives

00:36:05.660 --> 00:36:08.123
and erasing in order to add,

00:36:13.548 --> 00:36:17.020
in order to extend the
cultural readability

00:36:17.020 --> 00:36:19.620
of the document.

00:36:19.620 --> 00:36:22.170
So I'm wondering if you could speak

00:36:22.170 --> 00:36:25.620
why both of you have gone in the direction

00:36:25.620 --> 00:36:28.720
of redactions in a racial,

00:36:28.720 --> 00:36:33.160
how you think about it
when you are doing it,

00:36:33.160 --> 00:36:37.440
whether it's painting, the
white washing the painting

00:36:38.456 --> 00:36:43.456
or using tar to cover
the phase or to redact

00:36:44.070 --> 00:36:47.363
as you did in those, in
those pieces behind you.

00:36:49.317 --> 00:36:50.516
- Yeah, I was thinking about,

00:36:50.516 --> 00:36:52.240
first, I was thinking
about Percival Everett,

00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:53.303
great novel eraser.

00:36:54.320 --> 00:36:56.443
And one of the things that he does is,

00:36:57.700 --> 00:36:58.670
he creates this character

00:36:58.670 --> 00:37:00.650
that is writing a fictation novel.

00:37:00.650 --> 00:37:02.543
He's like a judge for a big award.

00:37:03.985 --> 00:37:05.090
And he's frustrated with the way

00:37:05.090 --> 00:37:08.860
in which people think about
the awards to come through.

00:37:08.860 --> 00:37:13.860
So he quickly pins of a
fake novel about black life

00:37:15.160 --> 00:37:16.180
and what he does,

00:37:16.180 --> 00:37:17.920
I think as a racist order to complexity

00:37:17.920 --> 00:37:20.250
out of black life within that novel

00:37:20.250 --> 00:37:21.950
and he expects it to flop,

00:37:21.950 --> 00:37:24.210
but then it ends up winning
the prize that he's judging

00:37:24.210 --> 00:37:27.080
and it ends up being
like wildly successful,

00:37:27.080 --> 00:37:29.200
more so than anything he's ever written.

00:37:29.200 --> 00:37:32.270
And so I tend to think of
redaction beyond a racial

00:37:32.270 --> 00:37:34.400
because I think about a racial.

00:37:34.400 --> 00:37:36.890
I think about a racial as the way in which

00:37:36.890 --> 00:37:40.103
you could cripple the
understanding of a subject

00:37:40.103 --> 00:37:43.870
by thinning it out to
justice as a stereotype.

00:37:43.870 --> 00:37:46.860
And I think the law as a whole,

00:37:46.860 --> 00:37:48.300
when you take that whole legal document,

00:37:48.300 --> 00:37:51.430
I think so often a law
as a whole does something

00:37:53.920 --> 00:37:55.750
to like flush great out understanding

00:37:55.750 --> 00:37:57.330
of really complex issues

00:37:57.330 --> 00:37:58.980
with all of the words they get added.

00:37:58.980 --> 00:38:01.830
So I like the notion of
subtraction as addition,

00:38:01.830 --> 00:38:04.500
because I was trying to
think of sort of independent

00:38:04.500 --> 00:38:06.450
and related to the work that me insight

00:38:07.305 --> 00:38:08.138
as we're thinking about, really.

00:38:08.138 --> 00:38:10.130
I was just trying to understand the law

00:38:10.130 --> 00:38:12.040
in such a way that I could
convey it to my folks.

00:38:12.040 --> 00:38:15.140
And I thought, what if I redacted,

00:38:15.140 --> 00:38:15.980
what if I just argued

00:38:15.980 --> 00:38:18.680
that a lot of this stuff doesn't matter?

00:38:18.680 --> 00:38:20.150
And I wasn't trying to do this in a way

00:38:20.150 --> 00:38:22.700
to transform the text
to say something else,

00:38:22.700 --> 00:38:26.830
but I was trying to do what
personal ever did, but flip it,

00:38:26.830 --> 00:38:28.690
Instead of writing the novel

00:38:28.690 --> 00:38:30.330
that makes you aware of

00:38:30.330 --> 00:38:35.330
all of the disingenuous ways we view life,

00:38:35.580 --> 00:38:38.070
what if I got rid of
all the illegal leaves

00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:41.603
to make you aware of what's
really at stake in these cases?

00:38:43.162 --> 00:38:45.230
And what was more troubling to me

00:38:45.230 --> 00:38:47.333
is how many pages I could get rid of.

00:38:48.186 --> 00:38:51.682
I would start with 70
pages and end up with 10,

00:38:51.682 --> 00:38:54.250
and I would start with 70
pages and ended up with eight.

00:38:54.250 --> 00:38:56.418
But the beauty of it was...

00:38:56.418 --> 00:38:57.400
And part of this wasn't just me,

00:38:57.400 --> 00:38:59.870
it was the lawyers who wrote these briefs,

00:38:59.870 --> 00:39:00.980
they thought it was really important

00:39:00.980 --> 00:39:04.280
to find a way to bring
to life the men and women

00:39:04.280 --> 00:39:06.700
who were suffered under
the system of money bail,

00:39:06.700 --> 00:39:09.490
or really under the system
of incarcerated people

00:39:09.490 --> 00:39:11.010
'cause they were too poor to pay.

00:39:11.010 --> 00:39:12.800
If I wanted to summarize it in a sentence,

00:39:12.800 --> 00:39:15.170
whether you're talking
about traffic tickets

00:39:15.170 --> 00:39:17.820
or bail or minor fines or fees.

00:39:17.820 --> 00:39:18.830
And it's cool though

00:39:18.830 --> 00:39:20.480
'cause there's a lawyer,
nobody understands

00:39:20.480 --> 00:39:23.290
the bandwidth of the
stuff I do with my family,

00:39:23.290 --> 00:39:25.350
but they understand things like freedom.

00:39:25.350 --> 00:39:28.020
And I think these pieces allow
me to sort of tell a story

00:39:28.020 --> 00:39:30.773
in such a way that my folks would get it.

00:39:33.190 --> 00:39:35.313
- Thank you, Titus?

00:39:35.313 --> 00:39:37.863
- I was interested in
redaction as Mark Macon.

00:39:39.996 --> 00:39:41.830
One of the things that
Dwayne and I talked about

00:39:41.830 --> 00:39:44.570
when we started working on this project

00:39:44.570 --> 00:39:48.020
is how do we collaborate on
something that doesn't diminish

00:39:48.020 --> 00:39:51.260
either the image or the text.

00:39:51.260 --> 00:39:54.810
And so usually when
you have collaborations

00:39:54.810 --> 00:39:57.260
with art and texts,

00:39:57.260 --> 00:40:01.413
sometimes one of those
things becomes the superior.

00:40:02.310 --> 00:40:04.720
And we wanted that not to happen.

00:40:04.720 --> 00:40:08.283
So the redaction itself,
the process had to be,

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:11.920
it had to be brush marks.

00:40:11.920 --> 00:40:14.840
It had to be a part of
the, the work itself,

00:40:14.840 --> 00:40:16.580
the painting itself.

00:40:16.580 --> 00:40:18.200
And so redaction just became

00:40:19.280 --> 00:40:20.670
the go-to language for us

00:40:20.670 --> 00:40:22.430
because it was a part of what we were

00:40:22.430 --> 00:40:26.760
or what I have been doing for a long time.

00:40:26.760 --> 00:40:29.657
redaction was for me in the painting

00:40:29.657 --> 00:40:31.510
you've been thinking
about the pain behind me.

00:40:31.510 --> 00:40:34.550
It's a way of focusing my perspective,

00:40:34.550 --> 00:40:37.010
focusing my attention,

00:40:37.010 --> 00:40:38.800
given myself the opportunity

00:40:38.800 --> 00:40:43.230
to move some other things in
the composition to the side

00:40:43.230 --> 00:40:46.560
while I explore a quiet idea

00:40:46.560 --> 00:40:48.230
that hasn't really had the opportunity

00:40:48.230 --> 00:40:50.830
to have the volume turned up on it.

00:40:50.830 --> 00:40:53.400
So in some ways, redacting
is the opportunity

00:40:53.400 --> 00:40:54.550
to turn the volume up

00:40:54.550 --> 00:40:56.833
on other parts of the composition.

00:40:59.980 --> 00:41:04.980
- Titus if the collaboration
caused you do wish

00:41:06.340 --> 00:41:11.340
to equalize image text
inside these pieces,

00:41:13.363 --> 00:41:15.410
how is that different
from when you're working

00:41:15.410 --> 00:41:20.213
on your own with the white
washing or the cutouts?

00:41:23.343 --> 00:41:25.343
- I have to trust,

00:41:25.343 --> 00:41:26.593
I have to trust

00:41:30.437 --> 00:41:32.310
that the redaction,

00:41:32.310 --> 00:41:37.310
that sort of redaction that
is dependent on the content

00:41:37.430 --> 00:41:39.890
of the poem that Duane is making

00:41:39.890 --> 00:41:42.043
is still a statically function.

00:41:45.170 --> 00:41:49.080
What we found is, it's all these words.

00:41:49.080 --> 00:41:54.080
It was never a problem,
we were redacting things.

00:41:54.580 --> 00:41:56.900
So the process of making these pieces...

00:41:57.800 --> 00:42:01.810
Dwayne, can you possibly share your screen

00:42:01.810 --> 00:42:03.680
and share that image

00:42:03.680 --> 00:42:05.753
of the two larger redaction pieces?

00:42:07.580 --> 00:42:12.580
So Dwayne takes the text
and the legal document,

00:42:12.590 --> 00:42:14.480
and then he redacts it down.

00:42:14.480 --> 00:42:16.560
- But you got to say too though.

00:42:16.560 --> 00:42:17.920
'Cause cause part of it is like,

00:42:17.920 --> 00:42:20.350
it don't work if you get a print

00:42:20.350 --> 00:42:22.110
and you have to read the next print

00:42:22.110 --> 00:42:23.940
to understand the content.

00:42:23.940 --> 00:42:27.120
So almost had to make it like a haiku.

00:42:27.120 --> 00:42:30.160
Like it's a sequence, but
each one has to stand alone.

00:42:30.160 --> 00:42:31.740
So if I was to read this one,

00:42:31.740 --> 00:42:36.300
when you get to $25 per
day cleaning a city,

00:42:36.300 --> 00:42:39.383
scrub the feces and
blood from jail floors.

00:42:40.460 --> 00:42:42.940
Like that's a wrap and we
could go to the next thing,

00:42:42.940 --> 00:42:45.310
but that's a complete idea in itself.

00:42:45.310 --> 00:42:48.630
Because again, it has to be
some kind of literal integrity

00:42:48.630 --> 00:42:51.733
with the words on a page
and the actual page itself.

00:42:53.328 --> 00:42:54.700
- Yeah, but in each of these pieces,

00:42:54.700 --> 00:42:57.453
each piece is actually four pieces.

00:42:58.722 --> 00:43:00.496
There's a portrait

00:43:00.496 --> 00:43:04.360
that I etched onto the
panel and then print

00:43:04.360 --> 00:43:07.520
then a second portrait
that etched and print.

00:43:07.520 --> 00:43:10.140
Then we silk screen the text

00:43:10.140 --> 00:43:13.450
and then we silk screen the redaction.

00:43:13.450 --> 00:43:15.890
And so we're seeing each layer

00:43:15.890 --> 00:43:20.110
of this individual piece come together.

00:43:20.110 --> 00:43:21.910
And after looking at it again and again,

00:43:21.910 --> 00:43:24.110
what we realized was the deconstruction

00:43:24.110 --> 00:43:25.890
was interesting in itself.

00:43:25.890 --> 00:43:27.500
So we started doing these pieces

00:43:27.500 --> 00:43:30.550
where we were showing
the individual layers

00:43:30.550 --> 00:43:33.190
because it helped you understand,

00:43:33.190 --> 00:43:36.040
it helps you understand
how the work functions

00:43:36.040 --> 00:43:37.310
when they're all together.

00:43:37.310 --> 00:43:40.040
We tried initially doing some redactions

00:43:40.040 --> 00:43:44.240
where it was more a digital redaction

00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:47.810
and we realized very quickly
that the hand was necessary.

00:43:47.810 --> 00:43:51.400
And so Dwayne redacted each
one of these lines by hand.

00:43:51.400 --> 00:43:54.630
It's subtle but it makes a huge difference

00:43:54.630 --> 00:43:59.630
that those lines are not
precise, that they are handmade.

00:43:59.670 --> 00:44:03.460
They angle slightly, they
wave, they undo undulate,

00:44:03.460 --> 00:44:05.030
that's all really important.

00:44:05.030 --> 00:44:07.010
So again, it allowed us,

00:44:07.010 --> 00:44:10.620
it allows the viewer, it
allows me as a first viewer

00:44:10.620 --> 00:44:12.660
to move back and forth between

00:44:12.660 --> 00:44:17.130
the faces and the redaction
as marks and the texts

00:44:17.130 --> 00:44:19.130
all sort of imbalanced with one another.

00:44:22.160 --> 00:44:24.120
- I also liked the fact
that from the get go,

00:44:24.120 --> 00:44:26.750
it suggests a kind of partial justice

00:44:28.996 --> 00:44:30.720
that the act of redaction means that

00:44:30.720 --> 00:44:34.030
we never bringing the
full force of the text

00:44:34.030 --> 00:44:37.673
to the plaintiff or to
the moment in a way.

00:44:39.077 --> 00:44:40.760
But I understand also

00:44:40.760 --> 00:44:45.023
that there were other
ways of thinking about it.

00:44:46.630 --> 00:44:50.850
Do either of you think about

00:44:55.150 --> 00:45:00.150
counter narratives as a strategy
for the work that the...

00:45:00.940 --> 00:45:03.977
Dwayne, you said you don't
care what white people think

00:45:03.977 --> 00:45:06.460
or it's not a frame
you're working against.

00:45:06.460 --> 00:45:09.790
But are you thinking as,

00:45:09.790 --> 00:45:13.423
it's 2020, we're making work all of us.

00:45:14.940 --> 00:45:17.430
And this is also true I
think for those people

00:45:17.430 --> 00:45:19.404
who are doing academic work.

00:45:19.404 --> 00:45:23.260
That there is a way in
which a thing has been told,

00:45:23.260 --> 00:45:26.333
a story has been told,
a picture has been set,

00:45:27.250 --> 00:45:29.150
and then there's another way to do it.

00:45:32.738 --> 00:45:37.738
And I feel like the choices
that both of you are making

00:45:39.796 --> 00:45:44.330
are actually critically engage to disrupt

00:45:48.217 --> 00:45:51.150
and prevent certain kinds of readings

00:45:52.609 --> 00:45:54.960
that here to for determined

00:45:54.960 --> 00:45:58.233
the narratives around certain subjects.

00:46:00.310 --> 00:46:03.140
- Maybe I think a more correct way

00:46:03.140 --> 00:46:05.057
I could have put my statement

00:46:05.057 --> 00:46:06.570
is that I can't what everybody thinks,

00:46:06.570 --> 00:46:10.400
but I'm not concerned
with trying to perform

00:46:10.400 --> 00:46:13.190
for a particular audience
at a particular time.

00:46:13.190 --> 00:46:15.230
And so it's almost as if

00:46:15.230 --> 00:46:20.230
the first poem I mentioned
"House of Unending".

00:46:20.600 --> 00:46:22.730
It's almost as if that poem,

00:46:22.730 --> 00:46:24.620
which has some moments

00:46:24.620 --> 00:46:27.530
that can be read as literally about me,

00:46:27.530 --> 00:46:28.660
but also has moments

00:46:28.660 --> 00:46:32.020
that can be obviously
read it's not about me.

00:46:32.020 --> 00:46:34.460
Almost as if that poem sets up a narrative

00:46:35.400 --> 00:46:39.280
that they're not gonna engage and get at

00:46:39.280 --> 00:46:41.750
with the Kamala Harris' piece.

00:46:41.750 --> 00:46:44.800
Even though when I write
"House of Unending",

00:46:44.800 --> 00:46:47.400
I'm not even thinking about
the Kamala Harris piece.

00:46:47.400 --> 00:46:48.650
So yeah, maybe that's true

00:46:48.650 --> 00:46:50.810
but I just don't think that...

00:46:50.810 --> 00:46:52.567
First of all, I think as a poet,

00:46:52.567 --> 00:46:56.220
the one beauty of poetry
is that any collection

00:46:56.220 --> 00:46:58.850
is filled with narratives
and counter narratives.

00:46:58.850 --> 00:47:01.050
But I think the other beauty of the poem

00:47:01.050 --> 00:47:03.910
is that unlike memoir

00:47:03.910 --> 00:47:06.350
is that I could like bring my imagination

00:47:06.350 --> 00:47:10.600
to explore and explore the sorta tension

00:47:10.600 --> 00:47:12.630
between a narrative and
a counter narrative.

00:47:12.630 --> 00:47:15.090
And to be quite frank often,

00:47:15.090 --> 00:47:17.930
I'm not certain where I'm positioned

00:47:17.930 --> 00:47:19.360
within that conversation.

00:47:19.360 --> 00:47:20.200
And I kinda think,

00:47:20.200 --> 00:47:22.910
and this is the difference
between me being an artist

00:47:22.910 --> 00:47:23.960
and me being an activist

00:47:23.960 --> 00:47:26.160
is when I'm representing
somebody on parole.

00:47:27.580 --> 00:47:31.296
You got murders and we're going to work

00:47:31.296 --> 00:47:32.836
to get you out of prison.

00:47:32.836 --> 00:47:34.920
And this is how we going
to tell this story,

00:47:34.920 --> 00:47:35.753
and this is who you are.

00:47:35.753 --> 00:47:37.860
And yeah, I'm honest about it.

00:47:37.860 --> 00:47:39.640
I think that they are...

00:47:39.640 --> 00:47:41.150
I believe in them.

00:47:41.150 --> 00:47:44.830
But if I didn't believe in
them, my narrative is set.

00:47:44.830 --> 00:47:46.313
And I think as an artist,

00:47:47.860 --> 00:47:51.970
my intentions don't have to drive

00:47:51.970 --> 00:47:54.050
what people take from the work.

00:47:54.050 --> 00:47:55.490
It's a certain kind of freedom

00:47:55.490 --> 00:47:57.530
and which I could do my thing

00:47:57.530 --> 00:47:59.560
and let it live in a world in a way

00:47:59.560 --> 00:48:04.240
that's not solely driven

00:48:04.240 --> 00:48:07.017
by what I want audience's response to be.

00:48:07.017 --> 00:48:09.720
And that's quite unlike I think

00:48:09.720 --> 00:48:12.330
the work I do when I
represent people in parole,

00:48:12.330 --> 00:48:16.600
and I think that's like
closer to activism demo art.

00:48:16.600 --> 00:48:18.380
'Cause when I represent you,

00:48:18.380 --> 00:48:20.600
I know what we want the outcome to be.

00:48:20.600 --> 00:48:22.517
And I know when I failed.

00:48:22.517 --> 00:48:24.030
The only time I feel as an artist,

00:48:24.030 --> 00:48:27.120
it's like when my son was like,
"Yeah, you can't say that."

00:48:27.120 --> 00:48:28.280
I'm like, "Huh?"

00:48:28.280 --> 00:48:30.077
He's like, "Yeah, you know
that line right there,

00:48:30.077 --> 00:48:31.675
"where you called yourself
a felon on like that.

00:48:31.675 --> 00:48:33.070
"I don't think that's true."

00:48:33.070 --> 00:48:34.854
Like that's when I feel as an artist

00:48:34.854 --> 00:48:37.770
because somebody who I
wasn't even thinking about it

00:48:37.770 --> 00:48:42.300
as my audience has like
cut into my thinking

00:48:42.300 --> 00:48:43.510
in a way that makes me feel like,

00:48:43.510 --> 00:48:45.380
yeah, I could have thought better.

00:48:45.380 --> 00:48:46.910
And that's the failure of an artist.

00:48:46.910 --> 00:48:48.710
I think at least that's
my failure of artists.

00:48:48.710 --> 00:48:50.270
When I find that, like,

00:48:50.270 --> 00:48:53.410
I could've thought better
in my art and I didn't.

00:48:53.410 --> 00:48:54.410
Although I kept my line

00:48:54.410 --> 00:48:56.100
'cause I ain't gonna let
no nine-year-old edit me.

00:48:56.100 --> 00:48:57.360
I'm just not with it.

00:48:58.489 --> 00:49:01.100
- I wanna throw this in there too.

00:49:01.100 --> 00:49:04.440
I wanna get to the counter
narrative in a second,

00:49:04.440 --> 00:49:08.140
but this is something that
Dwayne and I think about a lot.

00:49:08.140 --> 00:49:09.970
We're working on this
other project together,

00:49:09.970 --> 00:49:12.250
this film project, and
a couple of weeks ago,

00:49:12.250 --> 00:49:14.970
Dwayne was in the New York
Times for a piece that he did,

00:49:14.970 --> 00:49:18.270
and I was in the New York
Times for a piece that we did.

00:49:18.270 --> 00:49:21.180
Those pieces were reviewed
by different people,

00:49:21.180 --> 00:49:24.000
but who doesn't get to review our work.

00:49:24.000 --> 00:49:27.740
Our work generally is not
reviewed by our mothers

00:49:29.070 --> 00:49:31.660
and the people that
they sort of represent.

00:49:31.660 --> 00:49:33.933
And so what we did was

00:49:33.933 --> 00:49:37.034
we actually had our mothers
interview each other.

00:49:37.034 --> 00:49:41.120
That's to me, my mother's
thought on my work

00:49:41.120 --> 00:49:42.420
is sort of symbolic

00:49:42.420 --> 00:49:46.420
of like my community's thought
on my work in that way.

00:49:46.420 --> 00:49:49.273
That's something that I think about like,

00:49:49.273 --> 00:49:51.430
I think way more about

00:49:51.430 --> 00:49:53.210
what you and Dwayne think about my work

00:49:53.210 --> 00:49:55.300
than I think about what the New York Times

00:49:55.300 --> 00:49:57.240
or whoever's reviewing my
work says about our work.

00:49:57.240 --> 00:50:00.510
I'm fine with them hating everything I do.

00:50:00.510 --> 00:50:02.810
I'm not fine with y'all
hating everything I do

00:50:03.770 --> 00:50:05.640
because you know my intentions,

00:50:05.640 --> 00:50:06.473
you know me,

00:50:06.473 --> 00:50:09.654
you know what I'm trying
to get at in this thing.

00:50:09.654 --> 00:50:12.690
If I fail on that stage,

00:50:12.690 --> 00:50:17.130
that's a whole different kind of calculus.

00:50:17.130 --> 00:50:19.970
So I am looking at the counter narrative.

00:50:19.970 --> 00:50:21.980
That is something that I'm interested in,

00:50:21.980 --> 00:50:24.090
but I'm interested in it in the context

00:50:24.090 --> 00:50:28.620
of me as the first audience,
how do I wanna see it?

00:50:28.620 --> 00:50:30.220
And that evolved in me

00:50:30.220 --> 00:50:32.580
because I had to learn in graduate school

00:50:33.771 --> 00:50:36.600
that the professors critiqued

00:50:36.600 --> 00:50:38.010
what I was doing quite a bit.

00:50:38.010 --> 00:50:41.440
That's the nature of
the MFA program at Yale.

00:50:41.440 --> 00:50:44.520
And I didn't have a whole lot of allies

00:50:44.520 --> 00:50:47.120
when I started cutting
holes and paintings.

00:50:47.120 --> 00:50:48.740
I didn't have a whole lot of allies

00:50:48.740 --> 00:50:50.590
when I started Washington paintings.

00:50:50.590 --> 00:50:51.920
Those same professors now

00:50:51.920 --> 00:50:54.030
are very interested in what I'm doing,

00:50:54.030 --> 00:50:56.190
but at the time that's not it.

00:50:56.190 --> 00:50:58.330
So I had to figure out
whether I wanted it or not,

00:50:58.330 --> 00:51:02.205
whether it felt right to
me or not knowing that

00:51:02.205 --> 00:51:04.303
they may never get it.

00:51:05.850 --> 00:51:07.610
When I'm saying like,

00:51:07.610 --> 00:51:09.710
when I sort of pushed back on being

00:51:11.799 --> 00:51:12.720
a sort of activist,

00:51:12.720 --> 00:51:16.640
that's kind of where
some of that comes from,

00:51:16.640 --> 00:51:18.710
because I feel like it's necessary

00:51:18.710 --> 00:51:22.390
for me to prioritize my
conception of the piece,

00:51:22.390 --> 00:51:24.000
my understanding of the piece

00:51:24.000 --> 00:51:27.530
before it goes off into the world.

00:51:27.530 --> 00:51:28.627
- You know what's crazy though.

00:51:28.627 --> 00:51:29.770
And I should admit this,

00:51:29.770 --> 00:51:32.970
is I did a whole lot of
work too saying artists

00:51:32.970 --> 00:51:36.160
should work more with
activist organizations

00:51:36.160 --> 00:51:39.630
and non-profit organizations
and did some work raising money

00:51:39.630 --> 00:51:41.050
and trying to build those connections

00:51:41.050 --> 00:51:42.937
between those organizations or artists.

00:51:42.937 --> 00:51:45.410
But my point wasn't to turn
to artists into activist.

00:51:45.410 --> 00:51:46.760
My point was they acknowledged

00:51:46.760 --> 00:51:48.630
that we all live in this community,

00:51:48.630 --> 00:51:51.120
and that I do think that
the work of activists

00:51:51.120 --> 00:51:53.790
and the work of government officials

00:51:53.790 --> 00:51:55.150
would just be much better

00:51:55.150 --> 00:51:57.447
if it was more deeply engaged with art.

00:51:57.447 --> 00:51:59.290
And I do believe that you could do that

00:51:59.290 --> 00:52:03.250
without becoming propaganda for a machine.

00:52:03.250 --> 00:52:05.170
And I think that some
places across the country

00:52:05.170 --> 00:52:07.350
have begun to think about that.

00:52:07.350 --> 00:52:08.890
But you know, it's interesting,

00:52:08.890 --> 00:52:11.430
you talk to public
defenders and they be like

00:52:11.430 --> 00:52:12.863
a lesson before dying.

00:52:14.530 --> 00:52:15.763
Who is dead.

00:52:16.790 --> 00:52:20.907
And it's like, Oh, how do
we really do this work?

00:52:20.907 --> 00:52:23.840
And how do we really feel a
deep connection to the people

00:52:23.840 --> 00:52:27.960
who deeply do this work when
they literally sometimes

00:52:27.960 --> 00:52:30.670
will only support things
that you do and say

00:52:30.670 --> 00:52:33.410
that follows the company
line that they want to push.

00:52:33.410 --> 00:52:34.970
I got friends who were like,

00:52:34.970 --> 00:52:37.110
we were colleagues, we worked together

00:52:37.110 --> 00:52:38.880
and that sort of response
to my Time's piece.

00:52:38.880 --> 00:52:40.580
And I was like, all right,

00:52:40.580 --> 00:52:44.390
we aren't actually friends,
we aren't actually colleagues.

00:52:44.390 --> 00:52:46.383
What you wanted was,

00:52:47.250 --> 00:52:49.370
you wanted a puppet.

00:52:49.370 --> 00:52:51.850
And because I wouldn't refer it back

00:52:53.011 --> 00:52:55.610
to the things that you
were whispering in my ear,

00:52:55.610 --> 00:52:56.990
you don't wanna support the piece.

00:52:56.990 --> 00:52:58.840
You not retweeted it to your audience,

00:52:58.840 --> 00:53:00.240
you're not engaging with it.

00:53:01.307 --> 00:53:02.660
And I get it, I'm not mad,

00:53:02.660 --> 00:53:04.650
but now I recognize that
you're a part of the problem.

00:53:04.650 --> 00:53:07.620
Because as soon as my community's justice

00:53:07.620 --> 00:53:09.550
is not in your interests,

00:53:09.550 --> 00:53:12.260
then my community's justice
won't be on your agenda.

00:53:12.260 --> 00:53:15.060
And I think that that's one
of the things that I resist.

00:53:16.383 --> 00:53:18.932
Although I worked really
hard to get people to care.

00:53:18.932 --> 00:53:21.970
- Let me add one piece to that,
this political conversation,

00:53:21.970 --> 00:53:23.840
like just recognizing that

00:53:23.840 --> 00:53:26.360
it's all gonna be
political in some and that.

00:53:26.360 --> 00:53:29.830
I think that part of the reason why

00:53:29.830 --> 00:53:32.750
I also pushed back on this idea
of being a political artist

00:53:32.750 --> 00:53:36.140
is because I don't want the work

00:53:36.140 --> 00:53:38.613
to not be understood formally as well.

00:53:42.498 --> 00:53:43.331
You are a great poet

00:53:43.331 --> 00:53:45.140
not simply because of what you talk about,

00:53:45.140 --> 00:53:47.550
but the way you talk
about what you talk about,

00:53:47.550 --> 00:53:49.029
how you do it,

00:53:49.029 --> 00:53:50.810
the formal structures that you manipulate

00:53:50.810 --> 00:53:52.590
and use and change and alter,

00:53:52.590 --> 00:53:56.050
the rules that you use and
the rules that you break.

00:53:56.050 --> 00:53:58.660
When we talk about political art,

00:53:58.660 --> 00:54:01.770
the formal part of the art
goes out of the conversation.

00:54:01.770 --> 00:54:05.450
It's like, no, you didn't spend
20 years learning your craft

00:54:05.450 --> 00:54:06.630
and trying to figure this out

00:54:06.630 --> 00:54:09.320
and creating a language
that's functional for you.

00:54:09.320 --> 00:54:12.890
What it becomes is like a
conversation about the politics.

00:54:12.890 --> 00:54:15.470
And I know, going back to what Dwayne said

00:54:15.470 --> 00:54:18.300
about like first person
writing in the first person,

00:54:18.300 --> 00:54:19.660
I know that there are some times

00:54:19.660 --> 00:54:22.870
where like, you'll look
at a piece and you'll go,

00:54:22.870 --> 00:54:24.930
this is an amazing piece,

00:54:24.930 --> 00:54:27.440
I'm not sure if it fits the politics

00:54:27.440 --> 00:54:29.310
but this is an amazing piece.

00:54:29.310 --> 00:54:30.770
And this is something we struggle with

00:54:30.770 --> 00:54:32.830
when we look at art history itself.

00:54:32.830 --> 00:54:34.950
There are plenty of pieces that we look at

00:54:34.950 --> 00:54:37.580
and we go, politically, this is off.

00:54:37.580 --> 00:54:38.720
This is really off.

00:54:38.720 --> 00:54:39.610
And at the same time,

00:54:39.610 --> 00:54:41.240
I'm struggling because I recognize

00:54:41.240 --> 00:54:43.964
that that is masterfully executed.

00:54:43.964 --> 00:54:46.730
I think for me,

00:54:46.730 --> 00:54:48.770
I wanna live in that tension.

00:54:48.770 --> 00:54:50.440
I want there to be this difficult place.

00:54:50.440 --> 00:54:53.390
I don't wanna just hold on to one yes,

00:54:53.390 --> 00:54:54.950
Titus Kaphar a political artists

00:54:54.950 --> 00:54:57.800
or no Titus Kaphar completely formalist.

00:54:57.800 --> 00:55:00.493
I want to live in some
place in the middle.

00:55:04.751 --> 00:55:07.660
- That is a great place
to open up the discussion.

00:55:07.660 --> 00:55:12.660
We are at four o'clock and I
see no questions in my chat.

00:55:14.840 --> 00:55:17.340
Perhaps there's a private check Crystal

00:55:17.340 --> 00:55:19.000
that I'm supposed to go to,

00:55:19.000 --> 00:55:23.937
but in the chat function
there are no questions.

00:55:23.937 --> 00:55:25.720
- The people haven't
put in their questions,

00:55:25.720 --> 00:55:28.520
but this is the moment for them to start.

00:55:28.520 --> 00:55:32.250
They can say that a question
to you in the private chat,

00:55:32.250 --> 00:55:33.313
that would be great.

00:55:34.300 --> 00:55:36.240
But they will start doing that now.

00:55:36.240 --> 00:55:38.170
- While we wait for that to happen,

00:55:38.170 --> 00:55:41.130
maybe I can ask this one last question,

00:55:41.130 --> 00:55:46.090
because by talking about
both of you as artists,

00:55:47.150 --> 00:55:49.400
important artists, contemporary artists

00:55:54.150 --> 00:55:56.650
who engage issues of our time,

00:55:57.550 --> 00:55:59.160
it's not really an attempt

00:55:59.160 --> 00:56:03.471
to call you political artists.

00:56:03.471 --> 00:56:05.650
It's a way of saying,

00:56:05.650 --> 00:56:09.520
it's what is great about the work,

00:56:09.520 --> 00:56:14.520
is that it does not shy away
from the realities of our time

00:56:17.230 --> 00:56:19.800
and those realities engage

00:56:21.180 --> 00:56:24.290
many of the political hot topics of now.

00:56:24.290 --> 00:56:28.340
So it's just part of
what the entire picture

00:56:29.856 --> 00:56:31.743
of the life's work is.

00:56:36.480 --> 00:56:41.350
But I understand that we're
constantly in that fight

00:56:41.350 --> 00:56:45.950
not to have that word

00:56:45.950 --> 00:56:50.950
political engagement reduce the work

00:56:51.080 --> 00:56:54.810
and its achievements and it's excellence

00:56:54.810 --> 00:56:57.530
and craft and all of that.

00:56:57.530 --> 00:56:59.810
- But I would no doubt in a minute

00:56:59.810 --> 00:57:04.287
be the like poet in
residence at the national

00:57:04.287 --> 00:57:06.103
for Sherrilyn Ifill.

00:57:11.748 --> 00:57:14.440
I would like no doubt be
like if Bryan Stevenson

00:57:14.440 --> 00:57:15.273
was like, look, man,

00:57:15.273 --> 00:57:18.330
we needed a suite of poems
like Robyn Hayden did

00:57:18.330 --> 00:57:20.800
when he did that suite of seven poems

00:57:20.800 --> 00:57:22.320
called "Burning Spear".

00:57:22.320 --> 00:57:23.167
Man I'm hip to that.

00:57:23.167 --> 00:57:25.720
And I wanna commission you
to do something like that.

00:57:25.720 --> 00:57:27.230
So I guess what I mean

00:57:27.230 --> 00:57:30.710
is this is a kind of superficial
engagement with the work.

00:57:30.710 --> 00:57:33.450
I haven't been writing
the same poem since 1996,

00:57:33.450 --> 00:57:36.070
but I've had the same obsession since 1996

00:57:36.070 --> 00:57:37.550
because when they take a 16 year old

00:57:37.550 --> 00:57:39.400
and put them in solitary confinement

00:57:39.400 --> 00:57:41.350
for 10 days without giving them a bath,

00:57:41.350 --> 00:57:43.680
it tends to stay on your
mind for a long time.

00:57:43.680 --> 00:57:45.860
So I've had the same political obsessions

00:57:45.860 --> 00:57:49.843
'cause when you know men who
got sentenced into life at 15,

00:57:49.843 --> 00:57:51.720
and they stay your friends when you're 40,

00:57:51.720 --> 00:57:53.290
those become your obsessions.

00:57:53.290 --> 00:57:54.470
But I think one of the things

00:57:54.470 --> 00:57:56.040
I just wanna make clear about is,

00:57:56.040 --> 00:57:56.873
I'm not interested

00:57:56.873 --> 00:57:59.580
in a redactive
characterization of my work.

00:57:59.580 --> 00:58:03.534
If we wanna have a real back and forth

00:58:03.534 --> 00:58:05.810
about what it means to connect
the things that are right

00:58:05.810 --> 00:58:06.910
to the struggle,

00:58:06.910 --> 00:58:10.090
to end the over-incarceration
of the people in this country,

00:58:10.090 --> 00:58:11.980
if we wanna connect to
things that are right

00:58:11.980 --> 00:58:15.610
to achieve in social justice
among what school spectrums.

00:58:15.610 --> 00:58:17.810
That I'm really deeply invested in that.

00:58:17.810 --> 00:58:19.800
But what happens, I
think is a lot of times

00:58:19.800 --> 00:58:22.130
people just want a superficial engagement.

00:58:22.130 --> 00:58:24.354
You can turn on a news and you will not...

00:58:24.354 --> 00:58:27.730
I won't turn on the news
and see Claudia Rankine

00:58:27.730 --> 00:58:30.383
in the middle of the
discussion about addresses.

00:58:32.280 --> 00:58:35.170
Even something as basic
as microaggressions

00:58:35.170 --> 00:58:37.310
and people will like name drop your book

00:58:37.310 --> 00:58:39.370
and they will be like,

00:58:39.370 --> 00:58:40.510
and you will say

00:58:40.510 --> 00:58:42.910
we having this conversation
about Michael Brown.

00:58:43.780 --> 00:58:47.460
We've been talking about this
for a long time for decades.

00:58:47.460 --> 00:58:50.180
And my work wasn't in the
center of that conversation

00:58:50.180 --> 00:58:53.090
because the people who were
doing social justice work shows

00:58:53.090 --> 00:58:54.630
not to have it in a center compensation,

00:58:54.630 --> 00:58:56.650
or maybe not the people
doing social justice work,

00:58:56.650 --> 00:58:59.320
but just our inclination as a country.

00:58:59.320 --> 00:59:00.720
And so I guess what I mean is

00:59:00.720 --> 00:59:03.110
I don't wanna be the like step...

00:59:03.110 --> 00:59:04.120
I don't stepchildren.

00:59:04.120 --> 00:59:05.550
I don't wanna use that metaphor.

00:59:05.550 --> 00:59:07.620
I don't wanna be like the handmade,

00:59:07.620 --> 00:59:08.600
but if you say handmade,

00:59:08.600 --> 00:59:10.920
and that sounds really scary right now.

00:59:10.920 --> 00:59:12.940
I don't wanna be that off thing.

00:59:12.940 --> 00:59:14.730
I wanna be an integral part

00:59:14.730 --> 00:59:16.920
of the actual work that's going on

00:59:16.920 --> 00:59:18.523
in a way that I feel like,

00:59:19.930 --> 00:59:21.780
grapes of wrath was.

00:59:21.780 --> 00:59:26.320
In a way that I feel like
other texts in the past,

00:59:26.320 --> 00:59:29.750
like really existed as like a
fundamental part of the work

00:59:29.750 --> 00:59:31.160
that was being,

00:59:31.160 --> 00:59:31.993
and maybe I'm just being idealist

00:59:31.993 --> 00:59:34.320
and maybe they felt the same way I feel,

00:59:34.320 --> 00:59:36.280
but I sort of just feel like,

00:59:36.280 --> 00:59:37.400
I feel like I'm not,

00:59:37.400 --> 00:59:39.610
I feel like I'm always embarrassed

00:59:39.610 --> 00:59:44.610
about how little lawyers
I know know about...

00:59:44.630 --> 00:59:47.230
Your work was the first
work that somebody was like,

00:59:48.079 --> 00:59:50.550
"Yoh Dwayne have you
read this book "Citizen"?

00:59:50.550 --> 00:59:53.040
And I'm laughing, I'm like, all right,

00:59:53.040 --> 00:59:54.610
I'm glad you reading "Citizen",

00:59:54.610 --> 00:59:57.490
but what made it profound was
that was like the first time

00:59:57.490 --> 01:00:00.560
that people in law school was
carrying around poetry books.

01:00:00.560 --> 01:00:02.390
I was thinking about literature,
I was thinking about...

01:00:02.390 --> 01:00:05.310
And Titus' work is one
of the first times folks

01:00:05.310 --> 01:00:08.070
is like, yeah, man, this guy,

01:00:08.070 --> 01:00:10.010
poets that we know is like,

01:00:10.010 --> 01:00:11.600
you notice guy Titus Kaphar.

01:00:11.600 --> 01:00:15.370
And as being a part of the
landscape of the conversation.

01:00:15.370 --> 01:00:19.033
But I guess I just want
not to be an afterthought.

01:00:20.065 --> 01:00:21.328
And so that's why I want to claim that.

01:00:21.328 --> 01:00:22.920
- (indistinct) and then
move to the question.

01:00:22.920 --> 01:00:25.750
I was in Wyoming getting on
one of those little planes

01:00:27.006 --> 01:00:28.383
that terrify everybody.

01:00:29.816 --> 01:00:33.280
And this guy said to me, he's a poet.

01:00:33.280 --> 01:00:36.520
So it's not totally out of
the blue, but he said to me,

01:00:36.520 --> 01:00:40.450
"Have you heard of this
guy, Dwayne Betts?"

01:00:40.450 --> 01:00:42.410
And this was before, this was like,

01:00:42.410 --> 01:00:45.453
I think even before your
first book came out.

01:00:47.247 --> 01:00:51.500
I think you shouldn't underestimate

01:00:51.500 --> 01:00:53.350
the power of poetry out in the world.

01:00:54.525 --> 01:00:55.673
- Do sounds like a genius though.

01:00:56.860 --> 01:00:58.617
He sounds like an exceptional human being.

01:00:58.617 --> 01:01:00.160
- Yeah, but he is.

01:01:00.160 --> 01:01:03.277
Jenny Tang wants to know.

01:01:03.277 --> 01:01:06.847
"I wanted to ask about their
investments both of you

01:01:06.847 --> 01:01:11.457
"in portraiture and relation
of portraits to history

01:01:11.457 --> 01:01:14.070
"and subject hood and subjectivity."

01:01:14.070 --> 01:01:16.200
And so I guess it's both about

01:01:16.200 --> 01:01:17.510
the use of the first person.

01:01:17.510 --> 01:01:19.090
That would be another
way of thinking about it,

01:01:19.090 --> 01:01:20.523
but you want to start Titus?

01:01:26.696 --> 01:01:29.633
- My favorite historical
work is portraiture.

01:01:30.980 --> 01:01:33.230
Like I go to at first
when I'm at the museum,

01:01:35.596 --> 01:01:37.500
but you end up seeing,

01:01:37.500 --> 01:01:39.120
at least I ended up seeing people

01:01:39.120 --> 01:01:41.430
who don't really look like me

01:01:42.996 --> 01:01:45.436
and we're all arrogant to some degree,

01:01:45.436 --> 01:01:48.690
and we're all looking
for ourselves and images.

01:01:48.690 --> 01:01:53.690
And I wanted to understand
how to paint my skin.

01:01:54.320 --> 01:01:57.836
And so usually as an artist,

01:01:57.836 --> 01:01:59.520
what they tell you is
to go to the masters.

01:01:59.520 --> 01:02:01.190
And that's how you're supposed to learn

01:02:01.190 --> 01:02:02.473
is to go to the masters.

01:02:03.355 --> 01:02:05.390
But I recognize that the
masters hadn't explored

01:02:05.390 --> 01:02:06.957
on what I was trying to explore

01:02:07.931 --> 01:02:09.031
and that the occasions

01:02:10.075 --> 01:02:13.040
where there were
portraits of black people,

01:02:13.040 --> 01:02:15.723
there were more caricatures
than they were portraits.

01:02:16.810 --> 01:02:18.920
If you were painting someone who loved

01:02:18.920 --> 01:02:21.121
you wouldn't have painted them like that.

01:02:21.121 --> 01:02:23.260
And that was my experience
of looking at portraits

01:02:23.260 --> 01:02:24.093
in the museum.

01:02:26.150 --> 01:02:28.920
I spend a lot of time still thinking about

01:02:28.920 --> 01:02:30.660
the representation of people,

01:02:30.660 --> 01:02:32.910
not as a traditional portrait.

01:02:32.910 --> 01:02:34.900
I have one behind me,

01:02:34.900 --> 01:02:36.803
which is a character study,

01:02:37.900 --> 01:02:39.880
which is the big version of the small one

01:02:39.880 --> 01:02:42.743
that's in your spot body accurate.

01:02:45.150 --> 01:02:50.150
I also make portraiture where
you don't see the face at all,

01:02:51.190 --> 01:02:53.820
where you may just see this silver wet

01:02:53.820 --> 01:02:58.820
and it's a way of addressing
what wasn't there,

01:02:59.820 --> 01:03:00.830
what you didn't see,

01:03:00.830 --> 01:03:01.990
but it's still acknowledging

01:03:01.990 --> 01:03:03.950
that there was a black presence,

01:03:03.950 --> 01:03:08.590
even though I don't have the features

01:03:08.590 --> 01:03:10.868
to be able to articulate that.

01:03:10.868 --> 01:03:13.430
There are no existing
or historical records

01:03:13.430 --> 01:03:15.900
or details about those individuals.

01:03:15.900 --> 01:03:17.670
And therefore there's no way for me

01:03:17.670 --> 01:03:20.120
to like, put that down on canvas.

01:03:20.120 --> 01:03:21.590
Sometimes what that looks like

01:03:21.590 --> 01:03:25.060
is just a completely tarred out face

01:03:25.060 --> 01:03:28.210
or a silhouette of an individual

01:03:28.210 --> 01:03:31.313
that stands in for
someone I wish I did know.

01:03:34.710 --> 01:03:37.060
- I'm just gonna add Sarah Lewis's work

01:03:37.060 --> 01:03:38.810
around Frederick Douglas

01:03:38.810 --> 01:03:42.703
and how he was the most photographed men

01:03:44.070 --> 01:03:46.250
of the 19th century.

01:03:46.250 --> 01:03:49.590
And that his intention really was

01:03:49.590 --> 01:03:54.590
to put images of black
people, men out there

01:03:54.960 --> 01:03:56.393
using his own image,

01:03:57.440 --> 01:04:00.700
but just to proliferate, just do it

01:04:00.700 --> 01:04:02.690
so that when you close your eyes

01:04:02.690 --> 01:04:05.030
and you think what is
a black guy look like?

01:04:05.030 --> 01:04:07.280
It turns out he looks
like Frederick Douglas.

01:04:09.540 --> 01:04:14.540
So in a way that impulse to shift away

01:04:16.833 --> 01:04:20.900
from those images of daguerreotypes

01:04:20.900 --> 01:04:25.440
of enslaved people into a different image

01:04:25.440 --> 01:04:28.650
becomes the ones you see
walking in the street

01:04:28.650 --> 01:04:31.320
in (indistinct) becomes a...

01:04:31.320 --> 01:04:33.623
I'm gonna do the next question,

01:04:35.945 --> 01:04:37.120
and then Dwayne, you can take that

01:04:37.120 --> 01:04:39.280
and then we'll just switch back and forth

01:04:39.280 --> 01:04:41.750
so we can cover as many
questions as possible.

01:04:41.750 --> 01:04:44.463
This one, so Sarah,

01:04:45.535 --> 01:04:48.263
I'm gonna skip your
question and go to Jason's.

01:04:49.614 --> 01:04:50.973
"There are several,

01:04:51.907 --> 01:04:56.147
"what seemed to me
problematic dichotomies here

01:04:56.147 --> 01:04:58.657
"on the one hand Mr.
Betts draws a dichotomy

01:04:58.657 --> 01:05:02.067
"between the personal and the political,

01:05:02.067 --> 01:05:04.767
"and the personal can be very political,

01:05:04.767 --> 01:05:09.637
"but I wanna ask Mr. Kaphar
about a dichotomy he drew

01:05:09.637 --> 01:05:12.537
"which also seems to me to be real,

01:05:12.537 --> 01:05:16.227
"but also misleading between
the formal properties of art

01:05:16.227 --> 01:05:19.457
"and its excellence as political.

01:05:19.457 --> 01:05:22.417
"One of the ways in which Mr.
Kaphar's work is outstanding

01:05:22.417 --> 01:05:23.737
"is that the political message

01:05:23.737 --> 01:05:26.207
"is not didactic and overbearing.

01:05:26.207 --> 01:05:29.567
"It seems to me that here it's not just

01:05:29.567 --> 01:05:31.737
"that the formal excellence,

01:05:31.737 --> 01:05:34.737
"it's not just about
the formal excellence,"

01:05:35.930 --> 01:05:37.883
it's going off the screen,

01:05:40.087 --> 01:05:42.447
"plus an important political message.

01:05:42.447 --> 01:05:46.337
"The fact that his art is
not didactically political

01:05:46.337 --> 01:05:48.867
"has something to do with
an aesthetic properties

01:05:48.867 --> 01:05:50.847
"over and above the formal work.

01:05:50.847 --> 01:05:52.457
"Political excellence in arts

01:05:52.457 --> 01:05:54.817
"is not just having a good message.

01:05:54.817 --> 01:05:58.337
"I'm wondering if he would speak
to this aspect of the work,

01:05:58.337 --> 01:06:00.047
"how the aesthetics and the political

01:06:00.047 --> 01:06:03.810
"combined to make a political
message, not didactic."

01:06:03.810 --> 01:06:07.270
Dwayne, if you started with thinking

01:06:07.270 --> 01:06:10.480
a little bit about the
personal and political again,

01:06:10.480 --> 01:06:11.430
and then we moved

01:06:14.450 --> 01:06:19.007
to Titus' separation of
those two things as well.

01:06:21.954 --> 01:06:24.343
- But I think even a
separation counts for my work.

01:06:25.862 --> 01:06:28.070
You read my poems, you got blank verse,

01:06:28.070 --> 01:06:33.070
you have guzzles, you have
sestinas, you have calzones,

01:06:33.650 --> 01:06:36.080
you have a crown of sonnets.

01:06:36.080 --> 01:06:41.080
you got a lot of formal
attention to like...

01:06:41.330 --> 01:06:43.790
Arguably I'm like a legit formless,

01:06:43.790 --> 01:06:44.973
you know what I mean?

01:06:44.973 --> 01:06:46.360
I'm like, arguably,

01:06:46.360 --> 01:06:50.430
one might say that I
favor form more than that.

01:06:50.430 --> 01:06:53.566
I think though, thinking
about the personal

01:06:53.566 --> 01:06:54.711
and the political,

01:06:54.711 --> 01:06:57.790
and I think about the way
that art is perceived,

01:06:57.790 --> 01:07:00.430
I guess one of the distinctions
I'm just trying to draw

01:07:00.430 --> 01:07:03.930
is between, like I
frequently just don't know

01:07:03.930 --> 01:07:06.520
what I'm gonna say when I go to the page,

01:07:06.520 --> 01:07:09.060
and I'm just trying to
draw that distinction.

01:07:09.060 --> 01:07:12.380
I write, even Kamala Harris piece,

01:07:12.380 --> 01:07:15.440
I don't know what I'm gonna
say when I go to the page

01:07:15.440 --> 01:07:18.600
and a process of producing art

01:07:18.600 --> 01:07:20.810
is the one relying on some of

01:07:20.810 --> 01:07:23.850
the formal skills I have
to push me to a truth

01:07:23.850 --> 01:07:25.790
that I didn't have when I started.

01:07:25.790 --> 01:07:28.370
And I think too to try to reveal something

01:07:29.500 --> 01:07:32.590
about the thing that I'm writing about

01:07:32.590 --> 01:07:34.810
that astonishes me.

01:07:34.810 --> 01:07:39.660
So I guess my, I don't
know if the dichotomy

01:07:39.660 --> 01:07:41.360
is a real dichotomy on that,

01:07:41.360 --> 01:07:44.710
except I'm trying to distinguish between,

01:07:44.710 --> 01:07:47.370
I think like gossip and gospel.

01:07:47.370 --> 01:07:48.600
And I think gossip

01:07:48.600 --> 01:07:51.560
is what you trade with
friends on street corners

01:07:51.560 --> 01:07:53.310
and gospel is when you hear a story

01:07:53.310 --> 01:07:55.040
from a friend on a street corner,

01:07:55.040 --> 01:07:57.330
and you have to say it again,

01:07:57.330 --> 01:07:58.920
and you have to say it again

01:07:58.920 --> 01:08:00.180
in a meaningful saying that is

01:08:00.180 --> 01:08:03.500
because you think it just makes
the world feel a bit better

01:08:03.500 --> 01:08:05.863
by having told that story again.

01:08:05.863 --> 01:08:09.270
I like to believe that the
poacher rate does that,

01:08:09.270 --> 01:08:12.160
but I also like to
believe that it does that

01:08:12.160 --> 01:08:14.610
because I don't know what
I'm gonna say going in.

01:08:15.450 --> 01:08:20.399
I think what you're saying
that is incredibly important,

01:08:20.399 --> 01:08:23.940
and as a fellow poet, I totally
totally agree with this,

01:08:23.940 --> 01:08:27.650
is that the creation of art

01:08:27.650 --> 01:08:31.020
really doesn't have intention in it.

01:08:31.020 --> 01:08:33.520
And that's what makes it creation,

01:08:33.520 --> 01:08:34.870
that the act of creation

01:08:34.870 --> 01:08:36.870
is happening in the making of the thing.

01:08:37.988 --> 01:08:42.988
And intention has to do
with having an agenda,

01:08:43.290 --> 01:08:46.100
which is how we think
about political action

01:08:46.100 --> 01:08:49.120
and acting on that agenda
towards that agenda.

01:08:49.120 --> 01:08:53.470
That's why they say, like my
agenda here is women's choice.

01:08:53.470 --> 01:08:56.850
And then they go and do
the many different ways,

01:08:56.850 --> 01:08:59.420
approach that issue in
those different ways.

01:08:59.420 --> 01:09:00.290
What about you Titus?

01:09:00.290 --> 01:09:02.083
What are you thinking about-

01:09:03.940 --> 01:09:05.890
- I think the reason the
person sees the dichotomy

01:09:05.890 --> 01:09:09.550
is 'cause that's what I'm trying to say.

01:09:09.550 --> 01:09:13.830
I'm trying to say that
I'm trying to do both.

01:09:13.830 --> 01:09:15.310
I'm trying to exist in both.

01:09:15.310 --> 01:09:17.690
And as soon as you look
at something of mine

01:09:17.690 --> 01:09:21.510
and you think of it as a
political piece, stop doing that

01:09:21.510 --> 01:09:22.940
and think of it as a formal piece,

01:09:22.940 --> 01:09:24.000
then stop doing that,

01:09:24.000 --> 01:09:26.132
and then think of it as a political piece.

01:09:26.132 --> 01:09:28.860
I wanna be bouncing back
and forth between us.

01:09:28.860 --> 01:09:30.470
I don't want you to feel comfortable

01:09:30.470 --> 01:09:32.117
calling me a political artist,

01:09:32.117 --> 01:09:32.950
and I don't want you to feel comfortable

01:09:32.950 --> 01:09:34.130
calling me a formal artist.

01:09:34.130 --> 01:09:37.393
I want you to exist in
both of those space.

01:09:37.393 --> 01:09:40.680
It's difficult for us to hold
these two different ideas

01:09:40.680 --> 01:09:42.010
in our hands at the same time,

01:09:42.010 --> 01:09:43.660
but I'm asking you to do that.

01:09:43.660 --> 01:09:45.822
'Cause that's the way
I approach your work.

01:09:45.822 --> 01:09:48.140
And it does have to do
with this idea of process

01:09:48.140 --> 01:09:51.030
that if I'm in the studio,
when I'm making a painting,

01:09:51.030 --> 01:09:54.920
but in this painting,
what I politically believe

01:09:57.740 --> 01:09:59.120
is not highlighted,

01:09:59.120 --> 01:10:02.860
but formally the thing is blowing my mind,

01:10:02.860 --> 01:10:04.490
the painting is done.

01:10:04.490 --> 01:10:07.810
And so I have to wrestle
in myself with what to do

01:10:07.810 --> 01:10:10.450
with this other sort of political stuff

01:10:10.450 --> 01:10:11.693
that's like not working,

01:10:12.769 --> 01:10:15.240
but I have to sit with it.

01:10:15.240 --> 01:10:18.060
Because what I've learned from my process

01:10:18.060 --> 01:10:21.630
is what Dwayne is saying,
half the time I don't know.

01:10:21.630 --> 01:10:22.930
I just don't.

01:10:22.930 --> 01:10:25.010
The first time I balled up a painting,

01:10:25.010 --> 01:10:27.770
I had no idea what I was doing.

01:10:27.770 --> 01:10:29.070
No clue at all.

01:10:29.070 --> 01:10:30.830
It made no sense to me.

01:10:30.830 --> 01:10:34.230
I just knew I had to do it, period.

01:10:34.230 --> 01:10:37.070
And then I balled up the painting
and looked at it and went,

01:10:37.070 --> 01:10:38.250
I don't know what the fuck this is.

01:10:38.250 --> 01:10:39.083
Like, I don't even know

01:10:39.083 --> 01:10:40.300
what I'm supposed to be doing with this.

01:10:40.300 --> 01:10:43.190
And then I came back a week
later and saw the same thing

01:10:43.190 --> 01:10:47.090
and went, Oh my God, there it is.

01:10:47.090 --> 01:10:50.110
So I cannot be the kind of artist

01:10:50.110 --> 01:10:53.980
that puts arbitrary restrictions
on my creative process

01:10:53.980 --> 01:10:56.880
in order to fit some political agenda,

01:10:56.880 --> 01:11:00.610
because I would diminish
myself as the creator

01:11:00.610 --> 01:11:03.040
that I'm attempting to be.

01:11:03.040 --> 01:11:04.570
I'm still who I am.

01:11:04.570 --> 01:11:06.730
I still, the personal is political

01:11:06.730 --> 01:11:07.690
as we talked about before,

01:11:07.690 --> 01:11:11.970
I still hold the spiritual
ideas that I hold.

01:11:11.970 --> 01:11:14.580
I still believe the things that I believe,

01:11:14.580 --> 01:11:17.570
and that is going to find
its way into the work.

01:11:17.570 --> 01:11:19.330
And I trust that.

01:11:19.330 --> 01:11:20.620
I trust that.

01:11:20.620 --> 01:11:23.980
And to the degree that there
is like internal conflict

01:11:23.980 --> 01:11:27.260
in the work, I believe that
that's a valuable thing.

01:11:27.260 --> 01:11:30.420
Like there is, we are,
we are conflicted people.

01:11:30.420 --> 01:11:31.850
And if my work doesn't reflect

01:11:31.850 --> 01:11:33.540
some kind of internal conflict,

01:11:33.540 --> 01:11:36.303
then I'm not reflecting myself.

01:11:37.270 --> 01:11:38.580
- So then in a way

01:11:38.580 --> 01:11:40.800
and I agree with this,

01:11:40.800 --> 01:11:42.553
the word artist is a verb

01:11:44.305 --> 01:11:47.083
and it is constantly influx.

01:11:48.662 --> 01:11:53.662
I don't really have the same
thing about how political work,

01:11:54.860 --> 01:11:58.150
because I feel like what I'm...

01:11:58.150 --> 01:12:02.410
There's no way to pull out
the politics from the reality

01:12:02.410 --> 01:12:03.490
or from the life.

01:12:03.490 --> 01:12:05.140
You just cannot.

01:12:05.140 --> 01:12:09.390
It's like saying I can
put it on at five o'clock

01:12:09.390 --> 01:12:10.730
and take it off at three o'clock.

01:12:10.730 --> 01:12:12.120
I cannot, I cannot,

01:12:12.120 --> 01:12:14.283
especially not as a black woman, I cannot.

01:12:15.540 --> 01:12:19.330
There's no way for me to
go to the page and work

01:12:19.330 --> 01:12:22.600
without all of that being in the flux

01:12:22.600 --> 01:12:27.600
of the creative process of the activity

01:12:27.620 --> 01:12:29.593
of the verbness of the word artists,

01:12:31.600 --> 01:12:32.493
I would say that.

01:12:33.760 --> 01:12:37.600
- I wonder if there is a
parallel in the visual art world

01:12:37.600 --> 01:12:39.636
and poetry world.

01:12:39.636 --> 01:12:40.623
I don't know.

01:12:40.623 --> 01:12:45.490
But my experience at Yale
was zero conversation

01:12:45.490 --> 01:12:48.920
about the political aspect
of what I was doing,

01:12:48.920 --> 01:12:52.330
zero conversation about
the social implications

01:12:52.330 --> 01:12:53.400
of what I was doing.

01:12:53.400 --> 01:12:57.630
And then once in a while,
someone from another department

01:12:57.630 --> 01:13:00.790
would come in and try to have
a conversation about that

01:13:00.790 --> 01:13:01.950
in our critiques.

01:13:01.950 --> 01:13:03.270
And it would be very clear

01:13:03.270 --> 01:13:05.973
that the room was not prepared
for that conversation.

01:13:07.502 --> 01:13:10.080
And so what ends up
happening is there is a...

01:13:10.080 --> 01:13:12.100
And I'm not saying that this
is good, by the way, I'm just-

01:13:12.100 --> 01:13:13.040
- No, no, no, no, no.

01:13:13.040 --> 01:13:18.040
- There is a way in
which visual arts can...

01:13:18.810 --> 01:13:21.560
People will surely review the thing

01:13:21.560 --> 01:13:24.830
based on it's like formal aspects

01:13:24.830 --> 01:13:26.791
in the conversation that's had,

01:13:26.791 --> 01:13:29.900
this is a conversation with
abstract expressionism,

01:13:29.900 --> 01:13:32.170
and you're thinking about the cacooning.

01:13:32.170 --> 01:13:33.260
And you're thinking about...

01:13:33.260 --> 01:13:34.220
All of these things,

01:13:34.220 --> 01:13:36.540
we can have a very, very
long conversation about that.

01:13:36.540 --> 01:13:38.400
And not one time,

01:13:38.400 --> 01:13:41.370
not one time talk about
the black character,

01:13:41.370 --> 01:13:43.520
or we can have the opposite conversation

01:13:43.520 --> 01:13:44.740
where we only talk about

01:13:44.740 --> 01:13:46.620
the fact that there's a
black character there,

01:13:46.620 --> 01:13:47.740
and we don't talk about

01:13:47.740 --> 01:13:49.960
the fact that yes, in
fact, I am in conversation

01:13:49.960 --> 01:13:50.793
with the cacooning.

01:13:50.793 --> 01:13:53.260
I am having a conversation
about abstract expressionism-

01:13:53.260 --> 01:13:54.450
- Poets do that all the time.

01:13:54.450 --> 01:13:55.283
- Pardon.

01:13:55.283 --> 01:13:56.600
- Poets do that all the time.

01:13:57.570 --> 01:14:00.470
- But I think we're both
what we're talking about.

01:14:00.470 --> 01:14:02.320
I think when you say that.

01:14:02.320 --> 01:14:06.377
When you talk about the
historian, the art historians,

01:14:06.377 --> 01:14:07.770
the world of critique,

01:14:07.770 --> 01:14:10.450
we're talking about standards put in place

01:14:10.450 --> 01:14:13.140
by white people who have decided

01:14:13.140 --> 01:14:17.210
that as much as the work
looks like their work,

01:14:17.210 --> 01:14:18.173
that's how good it,

01:14:19.040 --> 01:14:23.640
and that's how excellence
is maintained and created.

01:14:23.640 --> 01:14:28.640
And so to have to refer to black people

01:14:28.660 --> 01:14:32.820
as a political force or as a human being,

01:14:32.820 --> 01:14:34.500
that's a whole nother...

01:14:34.500 --> 01:14:35.960
So you can't really...

01:14:35.960 --> 01:14:38.130
There's a great essay

01:14:38.130 --> 01:14:42.960
which might be of interest to
both of you by Denise morale.

01:14:42.960 --> 01:14:47.960
She talks about Manet's Olympia

01:14:48.570 --> 01:14:52.400
and how all of the critical
work done up until now

01:14:53.360 --> 01:14:58.150
gave a sentence to the
black woman in the painting.

01:14:58.150 --> 01:15:02.200
It said the black woman in
the painting and the cat

01:15:02.200 --> 01:15:05.500
have no significance, but if you...

01:15:05.500 --> 01:15:10.240
And then she went back and
looked at Manet's archives,

01:15:10.240 --> 01:15:12.903
he painted three paintings
of that black woman.

01:15:13.965 --> 01:15:17.570
He was interested in her
role in Paris at that time.

01:15:17.570 --> 01:15:19.990
But white artistsans

01:15:19.990 --> 01:15:24.530
in their myopic centering of whiteness

01:15:24.530 --> 01:15:29.311
dismissed even his intention,
even Manet's intention.

01:15:29.311 --> 01:15:31.961
- Yeah, I've read, I listened,
I watched the lecture.

01:15:32.861 --> 01:15:35.690
- (indistinct)

01:15:35.690 --> 01:15:36.523
- Her book is...

01:15:36.523 --> 01:15:37.480
I didn't read the book yet,

01:15:37.480 --> 01:15:38.313
but it looks fascinating.

01:15:38.313 --> 01:15:40.673
And I caught the lecture
that she gave about it.

01:15:42.830 --> 01:15:45.220
- But I'm gonna end
with the last question.

01:15:45.220 --> 01:15:48.807
Sarah Smith, "I would
love to know more about

01:15:48.807 --> 01:15:50.467
"what Titus mentioned

01:15:50.467 --> 01:15:53.187
"was the difference between how audiences

01:15:53.187 --> 01:15:55.417
"approach works of art.

01:15:55.417 --> 01:16:00.387
"Could they both say more
about the expectations

01:16:00.387 --> 01:16:02.807
"that audiences bring to visual art

01:16:02.807 --> 01:16:05.180
"versus the written word?"

01:16:05.180 --> 01:16:08.840
Separate question, "How do
audiences take into account

01:16:10.415 --> 01:16:12.507
"the breadth and length
of the artistic process

01:16:12.507 --> 01:16:14.337
"while also accounting for the ways

01:16:14.337 --> 01:16:17.537
"that the artists writer
changes over time?"

01:16:20.750 --> 01:16:22.623
So questions about-

01:16:23.638 --> 01:16:24.555
- Audience.

01:16:25.943 --> 01:16:29.260
- Audience and also the breadth

01:16:29.260 --> 01:16:33.720
of the uber of the artists.

01:16:33.720 --> 01:16:35.460
- I think the first question

01:16:35.460 --> 01:16:37.940
we've been talking about
the whole time really.

01:16:37.940 --> 01:16:40.120
- I think it's different
expectation though

01:16:40.120 --> 01:16:44.133
for painters and poets, I think.

01:16:44.980 --> 01:16:47.285
- I mean, don't neither one
of us have our audience.

01:16:47.285 --> 01:16:49.735
This is like the reality of it,

01:16:49.735 --> 01:16:51.013
we having this conversation-

01:16:51.013 --> 01:16:54.457
- I think we planned I think
the poets had no audience.

01:16:54.457 --> 01:16:57.047
- No painters ain't got
no audience, this is real.

01:16:57.047 --> 01:16:59.410
One of the things is like,

01:17:01.619 --> 01:17:04.972
to figure out how to have
people have art in their homes

01:17:04.972 --> 01:17:06.007
and think about art.

01:17:06.007 --> 01:17:07.301
And that's what made me
insight is doing a book

01:17:07.301 --> 01:17:09.150
for the redaction 'cause we like,

01:17:09.150 --> 01:17:10.730
we want the book in people's houses

01:17:10.730 --> 01:17:11.870
so that it's a part...

01:17:11.870 --> 01:17:16.870
Remember what "Crowns"
came out about 2005, 2006.

01:17:17.910 --> 01:17:19.300
And it had a companion book

01:17:19.300 --> 01:17:20.880
that was about black women heads.

01:17:20.880 --> 01:17:22.730
And I was working at a
bookstore during that time

01:17:22.730 --> 01:17:24.600
and it was flying off the shelf.

01:17:24.600 --> 01:17:27.393
We didn't have any art
books in a bookstore.

01:17:28.560 --> 01:17:30.720
But that book was flying off the shelves

01:17:30.720 --> 01:17:33.453
'cause people in the community
was really interested

01:17:33.453 --> 01:17:34.286
in this book about heads.

01:17:34.286 --> 01:17:36.300
I think Oprah might've
featured it or something.

01:17:36.300 --> 01:17:38.730
But I think the reality is
that like we both struggle,

01:17:38.730 --> 01:17:39.743
we have in a real audience.

01:17:39.743 --> 01:17:42.860
It's just that we got
to do the Wu Tang thing

01:17:42.860 --> 01:17:45.210
where we just produce one copy of our book

01:17:45.210 --> 01:17:46.883
and sell it for 15 grand.

01:17:49.319 --> 01:17:52.230
We satisfied with this 1000
print run, 2000 print run.

01:17:52.230 --> 01:17:53.690
I think we, we got to do bigger.

01:17:53.690 --> 01:17:56.180
But the last thing I'll
say about audience though,

01:17:56.180 --> 01:17:57.970
which is, I think is
part of what me and Titus

01:17:57.970 --> 01:18:00.550
have been doing with this
project and thinking about,

01:18:00.550 --> 01:18:01.680
as we produce this book

01:18:01.680 --> 01:18:04.360
and thinking about making it
accessible to a larger audience

01:18:04.360 --> 01:18:06.220
and having Pleading Freedoms

01:18:06.220 --> 01:18:07.910
and a neighborhood in New Haven

01:18:07.910 --> 01:18:09.840
where art usually doesn't exist

01:18:09.840 --> 01:18:11.277
is bringing it more to the people.

01:18:11.277 --> 01:18:12.160
And for poetry,

01:18:12.160 --> 01:18:14.113
I think for me my challenge is,

01:18:14.960 --> 01:18:17.300
how do I make sure I give a good show?

01:18:17.300 --> 01:18:19.710
And then how do I make that show exist

01:18:19.710 --> 01:18:24.710
in spaces that could like have
more people come to see it?

01:18:25.050 --> 01:18:26.560
And that is like a real challenge for me

01:18:26.560 --> 01:18:28.696
is to do something that's enjoyable.

01:18:28.696 --> 01:18:31.460
And so I turned felon
and two into a solo show

01:18:31.460 --> 01:18:36.150
with the intention of saying,
I'm gonna be on Broadway,

01:18:36.150 --> 01:18:37.080
with the intention of saying,

01:18:37.080 --> 01:18:38.790
how do you produce a piece

01:18:38.790 --> 01:18:40.920
that like demands an audience.

01:18:40.920 --> 01:18:41.753
Somebody in a season,

01:18:41.753 --> 01:18:43.480
like somebody flexing in the chat room,

01:18:43.480 --> 01:18:45.570
like I have a copy of "Crowns",

01:18:45.570 --> 01:18:46.810
and that's what I'm talking about,

01:18:46.810 --> 01:18:49.638
where you were like that book exists

01:18:49.638 --> 01:18:50.530
the way it was on the bookshelves

01:18:50.530 --> 01:18:51.830
like it was a best seller.

01:18:53.040 --> 01:18:55.170
And so anyway, I want my poetry to live

01:18:55.170 --> 01:18:56.727
and communicate to the
audience in that way.

01:18:56.727 --> 01:18:58.680
And I know I didn't exactly
answer the question,

01:18:58.680 --> 01:18:59.680
but I think

01:18:59.680 --> 01:19:02.750
I wanna cut through this
discussion of audience and say,

01:19:02.750 --> 01:19:04.890
what is the product that I produced,

01:19:04.890 --> 01:19:06.370
the art that I produce,

01:19:06.370 --> 01:19:08.920
what kind of audience does it demand?

01:19:08.920 --> 01:19:11.010
And my continued challenge as artists

01:19:11.010 --> 01:19:14.830
is to make a demand a bigger
and a broader audience

01:19:14.830 --> 01:19:15.663
so that they actually

01:19:15.663 --> 01:19:17.730
can be a part of the political landscape

01:19:17.730 --> 01:19:18.980
and the political conversation

01:19:18.980 --> 01:19:20.940
in a way that it might just be nipping

01:19:20.940 --> 01:19:23.100
at the outskirts right now.

01:19:23.100 --> 01:19:26.007
- Junior and Sandra both
have a copy of "Crown".

01:19:27.176 --> 01:19:28.877
So I think you're speaking.

01:19:28.877 --> 01:19:31.793
- Audiences is a weird thing.

01:19:34.124 --> 01:19:36.900
We're talking about the schools of thought

01:19:36.900 --> 01:19:39.130
that review our work.

01:19:39.130 --> 01:19:40.550
Let's be honest,

01:19:40.550 --> 01:19:42.770
I'm trained in a very European tradition

01:19:42.770 --> 01:19:45.180
of making paintings.

01:19:45.180 --> 01:19:47.850
And even though I push
against it in some ways,

01:19:47.850 --> 01:19:49.730
and people understand it
or don't understand it,

01:19:49.730 --> 01:19:52.680
faculty understand that or
don't understand that back then.

01:19:54.193 --> 01:19:56.050
That that is that, that is that language.

01:19:56.050 --> 01:19:59.350
And so I'm like trying to
take that and make it my own

01:19:59.350 --> 01:20:02.403
and do what I wanna do with it.

01:20:07.917 --> 01:20:10.730
But the audience question
is complicated for me now,

01:20:10.730 --> 01:20:13.270
because there's one way where I could say

01:20:13.270 --> 01:20:14.540
the audience of my paintings

01:20:14.540 --> 01:20:16.563
are the people who buy my paintings.

01:20:19.484 --> 01:20:21.573
No, no, not anymore.

01:20:23.316 --> 01:20:24.333
That can't be it.

01:20:26.140 --> 01:20:29.460
Because I come from that
tradition where as painters

01:20:29.460 --> 01:20:31.270
we're literally taught,

01:20:31.270 --> 01:20:33.190
it's weird 'cause this is contradiction.

01:20:33.190 --> 01:20:35.610
We're taught that you're
not supposed to be thinking

01:20:35.610 --> 01:20:37.190
about the audience.

01:20:37.190 --> 01:20:39.040
You're not supposed to
be thinking about that.

01:20:39.040 --> 01:20:41.450
You're supposed to be thinking about you

01:20:41.450 --> 01:20:42.980
and like how you think about the piece

01:20:42.980 --> 01:20:44.570
and how you come to the peace,

01:20:44.570 --> 01:20:46.250
is different from like theater

01:20:46.250 --> 01:20:48.060
where you're always
thinking about the audience

01:20:48.060 --> 01:20:50.040
and how like the audience
is going to receive

01:20:50.040 --> 01:20:51.410
and think about a piece.

01:20:51.410 --> 01:20:52.860
Where are you gonna be on the Stage

01:20:52.860 --> 01:20:54.940
in relationship to where
the audience is gonna be

01:20:54.940 --> 01:20:58.680
like, that's a part of
the art form itself.

01:20:58.680 --> 01:21:02.820
I now have to find different ways

01:21:02.820 --> 01:21:06.070
to get to my audience.

01:21:06.070 --> 01:21:07.830
First audience is me, always.

01:21:07.830 --> 01:21:09.990
I'm always my first audience.

01:21:09.990 --> 01:21:11.380
I'm never gonna make a thing

01:21:11.380 --> 01:21:15.190
that the rest of the world
thinks is amazing, but I hate it.

01:21:15.190 --> 01:21:16.170
I'm just not willing

01:21:16.170 --> 01:21:17.980
to put stuff out in the world like that.

01:21:17.980 --> 01:21:20.840
It doesn't matter to me what
everybody else thinks about.

01:21:20.840 --> 01:21:24.700
I'm working on finding
ways to get after myself

01:21:24.700 --> 01:21:25.630
as a first audience,

01:21:25.630 --> 01:21:30.330
get to this other, my folks
like my folks to see it

01:21:30.330 --> 01:21:31.740
because the work is now at a place

01:21:31.740 --> 01:21:36.740
where I'm happy to have the
success that I'm having,

01:21:36.750 --> 01:21:39.300
but it means there is a
limited number of people

01:21:39.300 --> 01:21:41.380
who are living with my work now.

01:21:41.380 --> 01:21:42.450
And those-

01:21:42.450 --> 01:21:44.690
- I will accept the piece though.

01:21:44.690 --> 01:21:47.606
If you want to expand that limited number

01:21:47.606 --> 01:21:48.979
- (indistinct) stop playing.

01:21:48.979 --> 01:21:51.562
- (indistinct)

01:21:55.290 --> 01:21:56.397
- We have to find ways,

01:21:56.397 --> 01:21:57.670
I have to find ways to reconnect.

01:21:57.670 --> 01:21:59.000
And so what do we do?

01:21:59.000 --> 01:22:01.118
We do these prints, we do these books.

01:22:01.118 --> 01:22:04.320
I'm constantly asked to do
these talks and lectures.

01:22:04.320 --> 01:22:08.620
And honestly, I evaluate
whether I do a talk in lecture

01:22:08.620 --> 01:22:12.260
based on the access of the
people who asked me to do it.

01:22:12.260 --> 01:22:15.210
If you have a lot of access,
I'm likely to say no,

01:22:15.210 --> 01:22:18.250
if you don't have access,
I'm likely to say yes,

01:22:18.250 --> 01:22:21.810
because like those often
are the communities

01:22:21.810 --> 01:22:25.240
that at very least reflect my experience

01:22:25.240 --> 01:22:28.450
which is where my quote
audience comes from.

01:22:28.450 --> 01:22:30.470
Even when thinking about myself.

01:22:30.470 --> 01:22:32.110
What would I have thought
about this growing up

01:22:32.110 --> 01:22:34.730
where I grew up 30, 40 years ago?

01:22:34.730 --> 01:22:36.360
Like what would have
might have experience,

01:22:36.360 --> 01:22:38.030
like we said before,

01:22:38.030 --> 01:22:39.260
what does mom think about this?

01:22:39.260 --> 01:22:40.093
What does mom..

01:22:40.093 --> 01:22:44.140
Like my mother's reaction
to Dwayne's piece,

01:22:44.140 --> 01:22:45.570
that was powerful.

01:22:45.570 --> 01:22:47.810
Way, way more impacting

01:22:47.810 --> 01:22:51.460
than reading Roberta
Smith review of my work.

01:22:51.460 --> 01:22:53.150
Way, way, way more of that.

01:22:57.070 --> 01:23:00.210
- I think that's a
powerful note to end on.

01:23:00.210 --> 01:23:03.123
And I think Claudia.

01:23:07.981 --> 01:23:09.720
- I have all these
questions I didn't ask him

01:23:09.720 --> 01:23:12.030
because you brought up refer to spit

01:23:12.030 --> 01:23:13.542
- (indistinct)

01:23:13.542 --> 01:23:16.360
- He called your work conceptual objects.

01:23:16.360 --> 01:23:17.670
How did you feel about that?

01:23:17.670 --> 01:23:19.160
- Who did? Me?

01:23:19.160 --> 01:23:20.373
- No, Roberta Smith.

01:23:21.630 --> 01:23:23.893
- She called them conceptual objects.

01:23:24.930 --> 01:23:28.020
- I think because they have cutouts.

01:23:28.020 --> 01:23:31.260
As I was thinking about
that phrase, I thought,

01:23:31.260 --> 01:23:34.150
I wonder what Titus is
thinking about that.

01:23:34.150 --> 01:23:39.150
- Because I know the
language of art review.

01:23:40.010 --> 01:23:41.830
I'm gonna accept the compliment

01:23:41.830 --> 01:23:46.653
she was trying to give me, how's that?

01:23:46.653 --> 01:23:50.230
I know what her intention was

01:23:50.230 --> 01:23:51.990
and we don't have to rehearse this

01:23:51.990 --> 01:23:55.330
but what I will say is that
it does go back to this idea

01:23:55.330 --> 01:23:59.210
of formal versus political.

01:23:59.210 --> 01:24:01.048
It goes back to that.

01:24:01.048 --> 01:24:04.250
She's elevating it in her mind,

01:24:04.250 --> 01:24:06.270
in her mind, that's what's happening.

01:24:06.270 --> 01:24:07.973
So I'll accept that compliment.

01:24:09.800 --> 01:24:13.360
- Because of the phrase brought
to me like a David Hammons

01:24:13.360 --> 01:24:17.500
and you're work and David
Hammond seems so different to me.

01:24:17.500 --> 01:24:20.660
That's why I was curious what you thought.

01:24:20.660 --> 01:24:22.973
But thank you both, thank you both.

01:24:22.973 --> 01:24:24.787
- Thank you, this was cool.

01:24:24.787 --> 01:24:26.640
It's actually important.

01:24:26.640 --> 01:24:27.700
I do think one of the reasons

01:24:27.700 --> 01:24:29.757
why I was excited about doing this

01:24:29.757 --> 01:24:30.700
is 'cause it was the three of us,

01:24:30.700 --> 01:24:35.639
and I think about how we
create historical records

01:24:35.639 --> 01:24:38.090
and how historical records from the past.

01:24:38.090 --> 01:24:42.370
You read a letter from Richard
Wright to Gwendolyn Brooks

01:24:42.370 --> 01:24:44.650
and he's like, man.

01:24:44.650 --> 01:24:47.920
Richard Wright is like
talking about the Guggenheim

01:24:47.920 --> 01:24:49.960
and you think it just means something

01:24:49.960 --> 01:24:52.787
to be a part a conversation
between the two of them.

01:24:52.787 --> 01:24:54.860
And it means a lot to be
a part of a conversation

01:24:54.860 --> 01:24:56.077
with the two of you.

01:24:56.077 --> 01:24:57.777
- Thank you Dwayne.

01:24:57.777 --> 01:25:01.100
- And it's amazing to be able
to witness that conversation.

01:25:01.100 --> 01:25:03.310
So I wanna thank you all three

01:25:03.310 --> 01:25:06.970
for agreeing to do it and
doing it so beautifully.

01:25:06.970 --> 01:25:09.280
And I know we're in this Zoom world,

01:25:09.280 --> 01:25:12.670
but I do like to take
a moment to ask people

01:25:12.670 --> 01:25:14.980
to unmute and to actually clap,

01:25:14.980 --> 01:25:18.650
because I think we need
to applaud you your work

01:25:18.650 --> 01:25:20.777
and this conversation.

01:25:20.777 --> 01:25:24.110
